#
# prima-materia — chaos magic: Hine, The Pseudonomicon
#
# The harvest of one work of the corpus of pmt:ChaosMagic, kept apart from
# traditions/chaos-magic.ttl, which declares the tradition and the work
# (pmw:ThePseudonomicon). Edition: Phil Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ:
# New Falcon, third revised edition 2004, third printing 2007); the copyright
# page names the first edition of 1994 (Chaos International, a limited
# edition of 300) and the second of 1997 (Dagon Productions). Read complete
# in the full-text layer of the open scan archive item
# the-pseudonomicon-hine-phil-2004-tempe-az-new-falcon-9781561841950-886b-
# 472313a-, 120465 characters; pages from the bare number lines at the foot
# of each page, several misread and fixed by sequence. Pages 13, 53 and 56-57
# are art pages; no foot number survives for p. 19, so the text between pp. 18
# and 20 is cited as pp. 19-20, and the stretch from the end of Dark Zones to
# the start of the Cthulhu Pathworking as pp. 56-58.
#
# Harvested under prima-materia#893 with two independent extraction passes,
# reconciled by offset and word overlap, then integrated by a third reader of
# the whole text, who added the nodes whose note says so. Every record was
# compared with the Hine nodes of traditions/chaos-magic-condensed-chaos.ttl,
# traditions/chaos-magic-oven-ready-chaos.ttl and
# traditions/chaos-magic-prime-chaos.ttl, whose Part IV (Cthulhu Mythos
# Magic) and the Great Old Ones of Condensed Chaos overlap this book: where an
# earlier Hine node carries the same claim, no node is written here; a node
# on the same topic in another form carries a note beginning "Differs from".
# Where Hine reports another author whose work is reachable (Lovecraft,
# Nietzsche), the node holds his ascription and the named work is not read
# through him. Signs of success and conditions of failure carry a note
# beginning "Counted as a sign of success:" or "Counted as a condition of
# failure:".
#



#
# Disclaimer (copyright page)
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMythosWorkDangerDisclaimerCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Danger of Mythos work: obsession, disintegration, shells"@en, "Gefahr der Mythos-Arbeit: Obsession, Zerfall, Schalen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Experienced magicians are said generally to agree that Cthulhu Mythos work is perilous because of a high risk of obsession, disintegration of the personality, or infestation by parasitic shells; the author releases the material anyway, holding that before Azathoth sanity and madness become inconsequential."@en ;
    skos:note "Self-warning against the book's own method, printed as its disclaimer."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCMythosEntitiesSaidRespondNormalCautela (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node reports the danger as an explanation Hine has met and disputes; here, in the book's own disclaimer, the agreement of experienced magicians is weighed and the material released anyway, on the ground that before Azathoth the question of sanity loses its weight."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGreatOldOnesForgottenEverPresent ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Disclaimer (copyright page), unnumbered; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Introduction
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicCannotBeConfined a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic cannot be confined"@en, "Magie lässt sich nicht eingrenzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine holds that magic does not stay within its rites: it spills over into other areas of life, may catch the unwary off guard, and propels the one practising into a liminal zone of sharpened sensibility in which other presences are felt; it is personal and bends one's perception of the world."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Introduction, p. i; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSignificanceAsPersonalGnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Significance as personal gnosis to be both cherished and challenged"@en, "Bedeutsamkeit als persönliche Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Validity of a magical experience is not decided rationally; significance is marked by some measure of revelation or gnosis that impels action, a personal truth that feels right, which should still be questioned while being cherished."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Introduction, p. i; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnObsessionCurseOfSignificanceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Obsession as the curse of sensitivity to significance"@en, "Obsession als Fluch der Bedeutungsempfindlichkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician's heightened sensitivity to significance carries the curse of obsession: drowning in meaning until every encounter, lyric, animal and friend seems cosmically significant; this is how fanatics are born, who suffer magic instead of enjoying it."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSignificanceAsPersonalGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Introduction, p. i; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOwnMeaningsNotReference a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Own meanings rather than reference works"@en, "Eigene Bedeutungen statt Nachschlagewerke"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since Lovecraft's narrators never state their revelations and his forbidden books leave only clues, the Mythos is a landscape of signs whose meanings the magician has to make for himself instead of looking them up; Hine offers the book as tentative notes from his own excursions."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Introduction, p. ii; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Cthulhu Madness
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEachGodBringsItsMadness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Every god carries its own madness"@en, "Jeder Gott bringt seinen eigenen Wahnsinn"@de ;
    skos:definition "To know a god and be accepted by it one must let its particular madness wash through oneself; this is absent from books of magic, partly because it is easily forgotten and partly because each must discover it personally."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnCthulhuNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Cthulhu"@en, "Cthulhu"@de ;
    skos:definition "Chief priest of the Old Ones, lying in death-dream in a sunken city; experienced by the author as vast, alien yet not alien, a slow heartbeat and a lidded eye peeling back through time; said to be all around us in trees, insects, bacteria and viruses, and to be dreaming the author."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCCthulhuNaming (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives Lovecraft's Cthulhu as lord of dreams and intermediary; here Cthulhu is the god as Hine met it in his own crisis, alien yet not alien, present in trees, insects and microbes, and dreaming him."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Cthulhu" ;
    pm:nameRole "high priest of the Old Ones; dreamer" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGaiaNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Gaia (as experienced, not New Age)"@en, "Gaia (erlebt, nicht New Age)"@de ;
    skos:definition "In a working with Gaia the author felt something huge pouring into his head: geological time, magma heat, grinding continents, insect buzz, nothing remotely human; explicitly not a benevolent New Age mother figure. Used to clarify his sense of Cthulhu."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Gaia" ;
    pm:nameRole "earth deity, vast and non-human" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 9; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNatureIsAlien a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Nature is alien; the Old Gods are everywhere"@en, "Die Natur ist fremd; die Alten Götter sind überall"@de ;
    skos:definition "No hidden dimensions or lost worlds are needed: the alien, theriomorphic presence of Cthulhu and the Old Gods is in nature itself (rock, coastlines, storms, plants, microbes), perceptible if one pauses to look and feel."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 10; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicianSelfCollapseFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magician-self collapses; banishing fails before Cthulhu"@en, "Magier-Selbst bricht zusammen; Bannen versagt"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the author's Cthulhu crisis his magician-self (claiming to command beings) collapsed; he could not banish the presence and felt he must not; signs were paranoia about windows and colours, sleeplessness, falling sensations, and obsessive self-protection habits such as checking plug sockets and avoiding trees."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of Hine's magician-self and of banishing before Cthulhu; he could not banish the presence, felt he must not, and the self that claimed to command such beings collapsed into fear and obsessive habits. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on the size of the god against the operator's pretension, and the passage ends in recovery."@en, "Narrated as the dark phase of an initiatory passage that Hine came through, not as a final failure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, pp. 10-11; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGodMadnessCasualtyCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Madness as an unmentioned casualty of magic"@en, "Wahnsinn als unerwähntes Opfer der Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author warns from his own case that magicians can become casualties of god-madness, a danger that magical books never mention; madness must not be accepted as a permanent option."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEachGodBringsItsMadness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 11; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnReEstablishingPatternsYieldsMeetingEye a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Re-establishing patterns yields riding the dream-pulse"@en, "Alltagsmuster führen zum Reiten des Traumpulses"@de ;
    skos:definition "Resuming ordinary patterns, after passing through Cthulhu-madness, led to steady meeting of the eye and a measure of power."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnReEstablishingOrdinaryPatterns ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMeetingTheLidlessEye ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 11; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnReEstablishingOrdinaryPatterns a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Re-establishing ordinary patterns after god-madness"@en, "Wiederaufnahme alltäglicher Muster nach Gottes-Wahnsinn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Steps: resume dropped daily patterns - eat regularly at roughly the right times, wash, go out for walks, talk to people - letting a self-preservation mechanism reassert itself until the environment stops being threatening."@en ;
    skos:note "The recovery step from the crisis of pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicianSelfCollapseFailure."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 11; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMeetingTheLidlessEye a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Meeting the lidless eye; riding the dream-pulse"@en, "Dem lidlosen Auge begegnen; den Traumpuls reiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "After passing through the crisis: meeting Cthulhu's eye steadily, dreams change, the thought of Cthulhu no longer fearful, ability to ride the dream-pulse, resistance collapsed and replaced by a measure of power; the eye is recognised as one's own I mirrored through fear."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 11; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnInitiatoryDescentAndReturn a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Initiatory descent into darkness and return"@en, "Initiatische Reise in die Dunkelheit und zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author frames his Cthulhu madness as the familiar initiatory theme of a descent into darkness and back out, charted by many books."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 11; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGematriaThreadIntoMadness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Gematria as a thread into Cthulhu madness"@en, "Gematria als Faden in den Cthulhu-Wahnsinn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Bounce gematria around, equating gods with concepts, using it as a thread to weave one's own Cthulhu madness and tip oneself into sub-schizoid significances; only if used appropriately."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGematriaYieldsOwnCthulhuMadness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Gematria yields Cthulhu madness"@en, "Gematria führt zu Cthulhu-Wahnsinn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Appropriately used gematria can become a thread toward one's own Cthulhu madness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGematriaThreadIntoMadness ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOwnCthulhuMadness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOwnCthulhuMadness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Own Cthulhu madness / sub-schizoid significance"@en, "Eigener Cthulhu-Wahnsinn / sub-schizoide Bedeutsamkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "A deliberately cultivated personal state of overflowing significance (Cthulhu madness) through which a god is known."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNecronomiconAsCipherForGnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Necronomicon as cipher for world-twisting gnosis"@en, "Das Necronomicon als Chiffre für weltverdrehende Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "There is no real Necronomicon that drives one mad; the point of the book is as a cipher for an experience that twists one's worldview and impels action in the fire of gnosis; whatever key one finds through madness will probably make little sense to others."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCPublishedNecronomiconsDisappointFailure (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node says no literal Necronomicon can match the imagination because horror must stay unrevealed; here the book is a cipher for the gnosis that twists one's world-view and drives one to act, and the key found through madness will make little sense to anyone else."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGnosisOfAGodsPresence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gnosis of a god's presence as the core of magic"@en, "Gnosis der Gegenwart eines Gottes als Kern der Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gnosis of a god's presence tears away veils, blows away character armour (which later re-forms), and leaves a lodged shard that drives one back for more; set rituals rarely approach it, whereas magic done in response to events or inner need does."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPossessionByDeity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Possession by a deity (Hecate)"@en, "Besessenheit durch eine Gottheit (Hekate)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine recalls having seen a priestess of witchcraft possessed by Hecate, with eyes that were not human."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeltNearnessOfOldOnes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Feeling the closeness of the Great Old Ones"@en, "Die Nähe der Großen Alten spüren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sensing the Old Ones close by at night, in wind, thunder and wild places."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnHillsideVigilYieldsNearnessOfOldOnes a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Hillside vigil yields felt presence of the Old Ones"@en, "Hügelwache führt zur Nähe der Alten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Staying in wild nature until nightfall brings the Old Ones near without calling."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnHillsideVigilUntilNightfall ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeltNearnessOfOldOnes ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnHillsideVigilUntilNightfall a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Staying out on a hillside until nightfall"@en, "Bis zur Nacht auf einem Hügel bleiben"@de ;
    skos:definition "To feel the Great Old Ones near: walk up a hillside, consider the age of the place, and stay there away from human habitation until night falls; out in wild nature one need not call things up."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 12; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnKinshipNotSummoning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Kinship rather than summoning: the rite vs the right"@en, "Verwandtschaft statt Beschwörung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A magician who feels kinship with the Old Ones need not call them; anyone can call, few from kinship. Facing a god and letting its madness change you forges a true bond; bonds with gods are two-way exchanges whose consequences may take years to manifest, gods being patient."@en ;
    skos:note "Timing: the consequences of a bond with a god may take years to show."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Cthulhu Madness, p. 14; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Invocations of Otherness
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPersonalSystemFromBareMythos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Personal system woven from the bare Mythos"@en, "Persönliches System aus dem kargen Mythos"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because the Mythos is bare of detail yet contains sacred landscape, dreams, sabbat, scrying and shape-shifting, a magician can build a uniquely personal system from experience with the entities; transmissibility matters little as long as it works."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCWhyMythosWorksGapsUnbindable (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node says the Mythos works because its gaps invite personal filling; here the bareness of the Mythos is the ground for weaving a personal system from experience with its entities, whose transmissibility hardly matters."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Uncharted Territory, p. 14; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRomanticGlamourCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Romantic glamour limits vision"@en, "Romantischer Glamour verengt den Blick"@de ;
    skos:definition "Holding a glamorous horror-movie image of Mythos magic as dangerous or tough is not wrong in itself, but it can limit one's vision and narrow the options available for work with these beings."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGreatOldOnesForgottenEverPresent ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Romantic Glamour, p. 15; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOutsiderStanceCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Outsider stance as self-deception"@en, "Außenseiterhaltung als Selbsttäuschung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Attraction to the occult can let one believe oneself a mighty adept while being inept on more solid planes; the author admits this was his own state when starting Mythos work and calls it a phase to grow out of."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGreatOldOnesForgottenEverPresent ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, The Outsider Stance, p. 15; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAntinomianSelfDeconditioning a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Antinomian self-deconditioning as vital to development"@en, "Antinomische Selbst-Dekonditionierung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A core Chaos Magick principle: continued use of antinomian techniques upon oneself is vital to magical development (Crowley's Libers, Liber Null); Mythos magick can contribute to an antinomian stance."@en ;
    skos:note "Crowley's Libers and Carroll's Liber Null are named as sources of the principle, without citation of passages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDisplayedWeirdnessFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Displayed weirdness defeats antinomian practice"@en, "Zur Schau gestellte Seltsamkeit vereitelt Antinomismus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Wearing one's weirdness on one's sleeve, dressing strangely or hailing Shub-Niggurath at people, often has the opposite of the intended effect."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of antinomian practice when weirdness is worn openly, which often has the opposite of the intended effect. No deadline; the rate word is often; the cause is put on the operator's display."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAntinomianBlendingInSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Sign of antinomian skill: blending in"@en, "Zeichen antinomischer Meisterschaft: Unauffälligkeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "A magician adept at antinomianism needs no display; like the most successful aliens in films, he blends into the crowd."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of the antinomian stance, whose skilled practitioner needs no display and blends into the crowd. Hine calls the criterion subtle; it is an absence, and nothing given separates it from plain conformity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesIndifferencePerspective a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Working from the Old Ones' indifference to humanity"@en, "Aus der Gleichgültigkeit der Alten heraus arbeiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Adopt the perspective of entities indifferent to human aims, seeing humanity as ants, in order to look at one's own desires, attitudes and motives from outside."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnIndifferencePerspectiveYieldsMetanoia a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Old-One perspective yields metanoia"@en, "Perspektive der Alten führt zu Metanoia"@de ;
    skos:definition "Working from the Old Ones' indifference is likely to give new insight into one's own desires."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesIndifferencePerspective ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMetanoiaInsightIntoDesires ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnIndifferenceToMysticismCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Indifference perspective can lead to mysticism"@en, "Gleichgültigkeitsperspektive kann zu Mystik führen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Working from the inhuman perspective can easily slide into world-renouncing mysticism through misreading aphorisms that equate a banquet with scraps; good magicians are said to avoid self-imposed poverty."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesIndifferencePerspective ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMetanoiaInsightIntoDesires a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Metanoia: new insight into own desires and motives"@en, "Metanoia: neue Einsicht in eigene Begierden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seeing oneself from a different perspective and gaining new insight into one's desires, attitudes and motivations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTransfigurationNewModeOfBeing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Transfiguration into a different mode of existence"@en, "Transfiguration in eine neue Seinsweise"@de ;
    skos:definition "Evolution into a different mode of existence for instance Deep One or Ghoul, bringing a new perspective, the capacity to dwell in other worlds, and self-sufficiency independent of others' judgements; likened to Spare's Self-Love."@en ;
    skos:note "Spare's principle of Self-Love is named as akin, with a pointer to Prime Chaos; the theme is taken up again under Transfigurations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Antinomianism, p. 16; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnBecomingTheBeast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming the Beast: Fear, Feel, Feed"@en, "Das Tier werden: Fürchten, Fühlen, Nähren"@de ;
    skos:definition "Three-stage integration of the Monstrous Souls: first fear the Beast, then feel it, then feed it; in practice, entering atavistic awareness so as to integrate it, under will, into the psyche."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine says elements of the three stages can be traced in Kenneth Grant's works and, more recently, in those of Linda Falorio and Mishlen Linden, and names the Tunnels of Set as one route; the stages are entered as the series pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBecomingTheBeastStages."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Becoming the Beast, p. 17; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBecomingTheBeastYieldsIntegration a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Fear/Feel/Feed yields integrated atavistic power"@en, "Fürchten/Fühlen/Nähren führt zur Integration"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three-stage Beast process integrates atavistic states and grants transformation."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnBecomingTheBeast ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnIntegrationOfAtavisticStates ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Becoming the Beast, p. 17; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnIntegrationOfAtavisticStates a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Integration of atavistic states under will"@en, "Integration atavistischer Zustände unter dem Willen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Atavistic, long-suppressed powers of the psyche brought within the psyche under will; one who returns from their world returns transformed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Becoming the Beast, p. 17; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAgainstMeshingOldOnesIntoSystems a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against meshing the Old Ones into existing systems"@en, "Gegen das Einfügen der Alten in bestehende Systeme"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author persistently disagrees with occultists who merge the Old Ones into correspondence maps and established systems, since their power rests on being undimensioned and unseen."@en ;
    skos:note "Only Hine's side is read: the occultists who merge the Old Ones into correspondence systems are not named."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCWhyMythosWorksGapsUnbindable (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node says the Old Ones cannot be bound into orthodox systems; here Hine states his persistent disagreement with attempts to fit them into existing systems and puts their power in their being undimensioned and unseen."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Becoming the Beast, p. 17; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNoBindingInTriangleCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "No binding in a triangle: abandon safety, maybe not return"@en, "Kein Binden im Dreieck: Sicherheit aufgeben"@de ;
    skos:definition "The psyche's dark terrors and the Old Ones cannot be fully bound in a triangle; one must give up all safety and go into their world, and return (if one returns) transformed."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnBecomingTheBeast ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Becoming the Beast, p. 17; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnWesternMagickIndoors a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Western magick is \"indoors\"; banishing unsuited to outdoors"@en, "Westliche Magie ist \"drinnen\""@de ;
    skos:definition "Western magical theory, like science fleeing nature, builds a Tower of Babel of symbols shielding the user from direct perception; the author has always felt procedures like the pentagram banishing (LBRP) unsuited to outdoor work. Deconditioning and antinomianism reduce perception mapped by conditioned reflex, making perception a sensuous communion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, p. 18; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnWideningAwarenessWithoutFocus a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Widening awareness without focus"@en, "Weitung der Wahrnehmung ohne Fokus"@de ;
    skos:definition "Develop the reverse of single-pointed sigil or mantra focus: widen awareness without particular focus until every aspect of the immediate environment is perceived as a possible medium of communion."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBPerceptionDynamic (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node says perception can be explored by narrowing or by widening; here unfocused widening is set against the single-pointed focus of sigils and mantras as a talent proper to Mythos work, until the whole surroundings become a medium of communion."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, p. 18; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnWideningAwarenessYieldsCommunion a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Unfocused widening yields communion perception"@en, "Ungerichtete Weitung führt zur Kommunion"@de ;
    skos:definition "Widening awareness without focus leads to perceiving the environment as medium for communion."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnWideningAwarenessWithoutFocus ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEnvironmentAsMediumOfCommunion ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, p. 18; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEnvironmentAsMediumOfCommunion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Perceiving the environment as medium of communion"@en, "Umgebung als Medium der Kommunion wahrnehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Global, uncategorised perception in which the whole surroundings become a medium for communion with the Old Ones."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, p. 18; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnWeekAwayFromUrbanLife a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "A week away from urban image-haze in silence"@en, "Eine Woche fern der Stadt in Stille"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spend roughly a week away from urban life, replacing internal word-dialogue with silence for long periods; notice perceptual changes; then translate these shifts back into awareness of city living."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, p. 18; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnWeekAwayYieldsPerceptionInCity a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Country silence yields altered perception"@en, "Ländliche Stille führt zu veränderter Wahrnehmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A week away with inner silence, translated back to the city, yields new perception including of the Old Ones."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnWeekAwayFromUrbanLife ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesPerceivedInCity ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesPerceivedInCity a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "New vistas of perception; the Old Ones brought into the city"@en, "Neue Wahrnehmungsweiten; die Alten in der Stadt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Perceptual changes and new vistas unfolding in urban life, carrying that perception of the Old Ones into urban life."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Earth Rites, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesIntoHumanLifeWave a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mythos work introduces the Old Ones into the human life-wave"@en, "Mythos-Arbeit bringt die Alten in die Lebenswelle"@de ;
    skos:definition "An often-proposed idea (Dion Fortune, Kenneth Grant, Pete Carroll) that aligning with this current unites chthonic primeval consciousness with future stellar magicks; the author holds it debatable below the abyss and irrelevant above it."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine says the idea can be seen in the writings of Pete Carroll, Kenneth Grant and Dion Fortune, citing no work; the node holds his report and his own reserve."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Invocations of Otherness, Transmutations, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# The Mythos and Magick
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAgainstGrimoireMythosTexts a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against grimoire-style Mythos texts and Golden Dawn approach"@en, "Gegen grimoireartige Mythos-Texte und Golden Dawn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dissatisfied with texts that turn the Mythos into a grimoire of rituals; it is not appropriate simply to fit existing procedures around it. Mythos sorcery is closer to shamanism, witchcraft survivals and voudoun than to Golden Dawn intellectualism and its descendants."@en ;
    skos:note "Only Hine's side is read: the grimoire-style Mythos texts he is dissatisfied with are not named."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAdeptHonedTheoriesInUse a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Adept as one whose theories-in-use need no set formulations"@en, "Adept als einer, dessen Gebrauchstheorien keiner festen Formeln mehr bedürfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Theories-of-Action are presumed true independently of experience and can be taught; Theories-in-Use are personal, largely unconscious guidelines learned only through live practice and cannot be taught. An Adept is one whose theories-in-use are so honed that set formulations are no longer needed."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondATheoryInUse (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node carries the distinction of theories-of-action and theories-in-use; here it is applied to Mythos work, the fledgling being taught theories-of-action and basic techniques, and the Adept defined as the magician whose theories-in-use are honed until no set formulation is needed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Theory and Application, pp. 19-20; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNoSetFormulations a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "No set formulations: techniques to apply at will"@en, "Keine festen Formeln: Techniken nach eigenem Ermessen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because Mythos work is so personal the author declines to give set ritual formulations; he examines appropriate techniques and leaves when and how to apply them to the reader."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Theory and Application, p. 21; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnStandardBanishingRitualSchema a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Standard banishing ritual (as described)"@en, "Standard-Bannungsritual (beschrieben)"@de ;
    skos:definition "The user stands at the centre (axis mundi), arranges at least four cardinal stations with a symbol of transcendence above, traces protective figures such as pentagrams or invokes protective spirits at each, using speech, movement and visualisation to link self, physical and astral space; performed to open and again to close a formal rite."@en ;
    skos:note "Described as the generic western schema, which Hine goes on to question for Mythos work."@en, "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicOrcCenteringRite (Oven-Ready Chaos, 1992): the 1992 node gives the centering rite as Hine's own common process before and after a working; here the general western banishing schema is described so that its assumptions can be questioned."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, On Banishing, pp. 21-22; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBanishingYieldsSafeSpace a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing yields safe space and role-taking"@en, "Bannen führt zu Schutzraum und Rollenübernahme"@de ;
    skos:definition "Banishing ritual is generally held to establish boundaries, demarcate magical consciousness and focus the magician."@en ;
    skos:note "Reported as what banishing is generally held to do."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnStandardBanishingRitualSchema ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBanishingSafeSpaceAndRole ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, On Banishing, p. 22; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBanishingSafeSpaceAndRole a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Safe demarcated space and the role of magician"@en, "Geschützter abgegrenzter Raum und Magierrolle"@de ;
    skos:definition "Said to create a fortified, cleansed space as precondition for magic, protect against malevolent forces, mark the shift from ordinary to magical consciousness (and back), and help one take on the magician's role."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, On Banishing, p. 22; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMundaneMagicalSplitNotAbsolute a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing enforces a non-absolute mundane/magical split"@en, "Bannen erzwingt eine nicht absolute Trennung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Banishing assumes magic happens only in prepared space and can be switched on and off; this mundane/magical distinction stems from a western intellectual tradition and may at some point stop matching one's experience; in Lovecraft the Old Ones are ever-present."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, On Banishing, p. 22; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDoorSwingsBothWaysCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "A door once opened swings both ways"@en, "Einmal geöffnet, schwingt die Tür in beide Richtungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Lovecraft is said continually to stress that once a door is opened it swings both ways."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine ascribes this to a work he names and does not cite by edition: H. P. Lovecraft's stories, as cited by Hine without naming a story. The node holds what The Pseudonomicon reports; the named work is reachable in its own right and is not read here, so the node is entered as Hine's ascription and not as a mediated reading of that work."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGreatOldOnesForgottenEverPresent ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, On Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAgainstSimonNecronomiconNoBanishing a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Against the Simon Necronomicon's \"no effective banishings\""@en, "Gegen das Simon-Necronomicon"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Simon Necronomicon warns that its invoked forces cannot effectively be banished; some critics take this literally, but the author agrees with those who expose it as magical hype; for him the issue is whether banishing is a useful concept for Lovecraft's landscape at all."@en ;
    skos:note "Only Hine's side is read: the Simon Necronomicon is quoted by him for one sentence, and the critics on either side are unnamed."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCMythosEntitiesSaidRespondNormalCautela (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node reports, without source, the explanation that Mythos entities ignore normal banishings; here Hine traces the claim to the Simon Necronomicon, calls it hype, and moves the question to whether banishing is a useful concept for Lovecraft's landscape at all."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, On Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBanishingIsContextual a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Banishing is contextual"@en, "Bannen ist kontextabhängig"@de ;
    skos:definition "Whether to open and close with formal banishing depends on the situation and can only be judged through practical experience; it helps to separate banishing's two modes, opening and closing, before considering alternatives."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Alternatives to Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningByLight a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Opening by transitions of light"@en, "Eröffnung durch Lichtwechsel"@de ;
    skos:definition "For indoor workings, mark the opening by moving from electric light into darkness, then to candlelight or firelight."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Alternatives to Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningBySound a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Opening by sound"@en, "Eröffnung durch Klang"@de ;
    skos:definition "Begin the event in silence into which the sound of a flute, gong or singing bowl gradually creeps; outdoors, sound is especially effective if made at some distance from the site."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Alternatives to Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningTransitionsYieldAtmosphere a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Light and sound transitions yield opening atmosphere"@en, "Licht- und Klangwechsel erzeugen Eröffnungsstimmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Simple light and sound transitions can do much to build tension and expectation suitable for opening."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningByLight, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningBySound ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningAtmosphere ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Alternatives to Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOpeningAtmosphere a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Atmosphere of tension and expectation at opening"@en, "Atmosphäre von Spannung und Erwartung"@de ;
    skos:definition "A subtle shift in mood marking transition, building tension and heightened expectation in celebrants, fitting for the opening of a magical event."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Alternatives to Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSilentMovementGestureOpening a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Silent movement and gesture for solo opening"@en, "Stille Bewegung und Geste für Einzelpraktiker"@de ;
    skos:definition "Solo practitioners can develop personal patterns of silent movement and gesture as alternatives to formal ritual (Steve Wilson's Chaos Ritual, 1994, recommended for devising postures and gestures)."@en ;
    skos:note "Steve Wilson, Chaos Ritual (1994), is recommended for exercises in devising postures and gestures."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Alternatives to Banishing, p. 23; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicalEventsVersusRituals a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magical events versus rituals"@en, "Magische Ereignisse vs. Rituale"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rituals are structured with distinct beginnings and ends; magical events are fuzzy-edged, need not be intentional, may trigger or be triggered by rituals, and their trigger may be seen only in retrospect. Rituals should be reported within the practitioner's personal context and consequences, not as bare recipe-scripts."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Events as opposed to rituals, p. 24; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnOrchestratingByVoiceAndGesture a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Orchestrating by tone of voice and gesture"@en, "Inszenierung durch Stimmton und Geste"@de ;
    skos:definition "Attend to the emotions fitting the event: use tone of voice (hushed vs firm and authoritative) to raise expectancy or imply an ending; remember even a formal banishing combines movement, gesture and speech, at least as important as its symbols."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Orchestrating events, p. 24; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnLengthyRecoveryCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Recovery from intense events may be lengthy"@en, "Erholung von intensiven Ereignissen kann lange dauern"@de ;
    skos:definition "Formal closing breeds the false belief that magic stops and all are back to normal; coming down from an intense event may be lengthy, and the author has seen leaders assume normality while participants were clearly not recovered."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnStandardBanishingRitualSchema ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 25; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnImmediateClosure a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Immediate closure"@en, "Unmittelbarer Abschluss"@de ;
    skos:definition "Demarcate the end of an event and wind down: gestures and declarations of closure, taking down props, writing immediate perceptions in a diary, talking the event over with fellow participants."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 25; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnLongTermClosure a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Long-term closure: integration into daily life"@en, "Langfristiger Abschluss: Integration in den Alltag"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deal with the event's effects on you by personally integrating and assimilating them into day-to-day life; necessarily different for each individual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 25; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEventSuccessNewIdeasSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Success of an event: new ideas and reconnection"@en, "Erfolg eines Ereignisses: neue Ideen und Wiederverbindung"@de ;
    skos:definition "For the author a measure of an event's success is that it generates fresh ideas and links between concepts and a felt reconnection with the world."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of a magical event, that it yields fresh ideas, links between concepts and a renewed sense of connection with the world. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline, and nothing given separates it from the after-effects of any absorbing experience, which Hine himself likens to a good film or play."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 25; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnClosureAndAssimilation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Closure and assimilation of a magical event"@en, "Abschluss und Aneignung eines magischen Ereignisses"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ending an event and assimilating its effects into ordinary life."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 25; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBanishingInsufficientForClosureFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Formal banishing insufficient for closure"@en, "Formales Bannen reicht zum Abschluss nicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the author's experience formal ritual banishings have at times not been sufficient by themselves to achieve closure (and at other times seemed inappropriate)."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of formal banishing as a means of closure, which at times was not enough by itself to bring participants back. No deadline; the rate word is at times; the cause is put on the method's assumption that the magic stops once the rite is closed."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 26; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnBeachEventDreamContactCthulhu a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Beach event for dream-contact with Cthulhu"@en, "Strandereignis für Traumkontakt mit Cthulhu"@de ;
    skos:definition "Open by reading Lovecraft's The Call of Cthulhu; walk silently along the shore, listening to the waves and reflecting on the ocean's vastness and hidden mysteries; at a promontory one member leads a guided visualisation; walk back collecting pebbles, paddling, discussing rocks; no formal banishing, so the ocean atmosphere is kept; recall the event before sleep to stimulate dreams of Cthulhu."@en ;
    skos:note "Reported group event; Hine's own part in it is not stated. Banishing was left out on purpose."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 26; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBeachEventYieldsDreamContact a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Beach event aims at dreams of Cthulhu"@en, "Strandereignis zielt auf Cthulhu-Träume"@de ;
    skos:definition "Retaining the ocean atmosphere and evoking memories of the event before sleep is meant to try to stimulate dreams of Cthulhu."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnBeachEventDreamContactCthulhu ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamContactWithCthulhu ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 26; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamContactWithCthulhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Individual and group dream-contact with Cthulhu"@en, "Einzel- und Gruppen-Traumkontakt mit Cthulhu"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dreams of Cthulhu stimulated by retaining and recalling a felt link with Cthulhu and the deep sea before sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 26; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnInformalClosureAfterLooseEntity a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Informal closure after a loose entity (anecdote)"@en, "Informeller Abschluss nach losem Wesen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When a participant felt an alien entity was loose in the temple and the group argued over banishing, it was proposed that the argument itself had dispelled the atmosphere; lights up, props down, drinks and level-headed debate sufficed; those wanting to close off could do it in their own manner, those wanting to keep the weird connection could maintain or enhance it."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine sided with those who would have let the entity turn up outside the ritual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 26; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicBringsChangeNotAscentCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Magic brings change, not usually upward"@en, "Magie bringt Wandel, meist nicht aufwärts"@de ;
    skos:definition "Practising magic leads to change, which is usually not the portrayed gradual ascent to higher states; even gentle New Age practice can unsettle one. Tales of madness blamed on Mythos work or improper banishing are too pat."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCAgainstViewMythosWorkUniquely (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node answers the view that Mythos work is uniquely dangerous by saying any system can lead to madness and change is the point of magic; here Hine adds that change is usually not the portrayed ascent to higher states, and calls tales that blame madness on the Mythos or on improper banishing too pat."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicalEventsVersusRituals ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnEnteringLiminalGnosis a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Entering the Liminal Gnosis"@en, "Eintritt in die Liminale Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Lie down and relax, for example after physical exertion, letting thoughts, images, sounds and visions well up; do not direct them but relax into them so that a coherent thread emerges by itself. Learn to recognise the various routes into the state, deliberate or accidental."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnResponsibilityNotBlamingMagic a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Responsibility rather than blaming the magic"@en, "Verantwortung statt die Magie zu beschuldigen"@de ;
    skos:definition "When magical work leaves us behaving badly, the issue is to accept responsibility afterwards instead of blaming the work or an entity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Achieving closure, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnLiminalGnosis a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Liminal Gnosis"@en, "Liminale Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "A passive state in which images and visions well up; umbrella for willed dreaming, scrying and astral (virtual) exploration; extends to perceiving strange sounds and fleeting presences at the edge of vision."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBLiminalPerception (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node names liminal perception as the borderline state any disturbance of linear awareness brings; here the Liminal Gnosis is a passive state entered by relaxing and not directing what wells up, and the umbrella term for willed dreaming, scrying and astral work in the Mythos."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, The Liminal Gnosis, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnLiminalRelaxationYieldsLiminalGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Liminal relaxation yields Liminal Gnosis"@en, "Liminale Entspannung führt zur Liminalen Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The state can be accessed by relaxing after exertion and not directing the visions."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnEnteringLiminalGnosis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnLiminalGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, The Liminal Gnosis, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnImaginingMythosLocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Imagining a Mythos location to launch journeys"@en, "Vorstellen eines Mythos-Ortes als Reisestart"@de ;
    skos:definition "In the liminal state, imagine a particular location for instance Kadath (Cold Waste) or Leng's Monastery to trigger dream or astral journeys."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, The Liminal Gnosis, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnImaginedLocationYieldsJourney a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Imagined location yields dream/astral journey"@en, "Vorgestellter Ort führt zur Reise"@de ;
    skos:definition "Imagining a Mythos location in the liminal state can trigger dream or astral journeys."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnImaginingMythosLocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamAstralJourneysMythos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, The Liminal Gnosis, p. 27; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamAstralJourneysMythos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dream and astral journeys to Mythos realms"@en, "Traum- und Astralreisen in Mythos-Reiche"@de ;
    skos:definition "Journeys in dream or astral (virtual) space to Mythos locations."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, The Liminal Gnosis, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnMythosTaleBeforeSleep a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mythos tale or imagined location before sleep"@en, "Mythos-Erzählung oder Ort vor dem Schlafen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Read a Cthulhu Mythos tale, or vividly imagine a Mythos location such as Kadath, before sleep."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondBBasicDreamControl (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 practice holds a scene lightly while falling asleep or uses a sigil; here a Mythos tale is read, or a Mythos place such as Kadath vividly imagined, before sleep."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Methods of Dream Control, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamDiaryEssentialDreamControlPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Dream diary essential to dream-control work"@en, "Traumtagebuch unerlässlich für Traumarbeit"@de ;
    skos:definition "A dream diary is said to be obviously essential to dream-control work."@en ;
    skos:note "Called essential, but no consequence of leaving it out is given."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBDreamDiaryFirstStepDreamPrerequisite (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node names the dream record the first step of dream magic; here it is called essential to Mythos dream-control work."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicCondBKeepingDreamDiary ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicCondBBasicDreamControl, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnMythosTaleBeforeSleep, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnScentLinkedDreamTrigger, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnContinuingDreamOnEdgeOfSleep ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Methods of Dream Control, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnScentLinkedDreamTrigger a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Scent-linked dream triggering"@en, "Duftgekoppelte Traumauslösung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Link a particular experience (e.g. an astral descent to R'Lyeh made while awake) with a particular scent; then evaporate that scent on an oil fragrancer by the bed to trigger controlled dreaming."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondBScentDreamLink (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 practice shares one perfume between two people as a telepathic link; here a scent is tied to one's own experience, such as a waking descent to R'Lyeh, and evaporated by the bed to trigger controlled dreaming."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Methods of Dream Control, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamTechniquesYieldControlledDreaming a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sigils, tales, scents and replay yield controlled dreaming"@en, "Sigillen, Erzählungen, Düfte erzeugen Traumsteuerung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Sigils before sleep, Mythos tales or imagined locations, scent association and edge-of-sleep replay each can produce controlled or continued dreaming."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCondBBasicDreamControl, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnMythosTaleBeforeSleep, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnScentLinkedDreamTrigger, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnContinuingDreamOnEdgeOfSleep ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnControlledDreaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Methods of Dream Control, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnControlledDreaming a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Controlled, evocative dreaming"@en, "Gesteuertes, evokatives Träumen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dream control: dreams steered toward chosen Mythos content, or dream sequences continued."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Methods of Dream Control, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnContinuingDreamOnEdgeOfSleep a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Continuing dream material on the edge of sleep"@en, "Traummaterial an der Schlafschwelle fortsetzen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Take sigils, images and objects found in dreams for further exploration; replay a striking dream-sequence as a conscious pathworking on the edge of sleep so it may continue in dreaming."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCDreamscaping (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 practice builds meditated image sequences from dreams with consistent Mythos elements and follows them into sleep over time; here any striking dream sequence, or a sigil, image or object found in a dream, is replayed as a pathworking on the edge of sleep so that the dream may continue."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Methods of Dream Control, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearParanoiaAsSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fear, neurosis, paranoia as sign of progress"@en, "Angst, Neurose, Paranoia als Fortschrittszeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fear, neurosis and near-paranoid states occur regularly in Mythos work; the author would recognise them as signs of successfully engaging this region of experience."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of getting to grips with Mythos work, recognised by Hine in fear, neurosis and borderline paranoia that occur regularly. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline; the next sentence says the same states may run out of control, so the sign is not told apart from the danger."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCCreepingParanoiaMythosReadingCautela (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives creeping paranoia from Mythos reading as a caution; here Hine counts fear and paranoia as signs of success, adding that they can get out of hand."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Emotional Engineering, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearStatesOutOfControlCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Fear and paranoia states may run out of control"@en, "Angst- und Paranoiazustände können entgleisen"@de ;
    skos:definition "These states of fear and paranoia can run out of control."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearParanoiaAsSign ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Emotional Engineering, p. 28; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearExcitatoryGnosisParanoiaPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fear as excitatory gnosis, paranoia as heightened perception"@en, "Angst als erregende Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once cognitive associations and projected fantasies are removed, fear turns into excitatory gnosis and paranoia into heightened perception; Liber Null's ego-deconstructive practices are useful here."@en ;
    skos:note "The ego-deconstructive practices of Carroll's Liber Null are named as useful here and not described."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Emotional Engineering, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEmotionAsBodilySensation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Emotion as bare bodily sensation, usable state"@en, "Gefühl als bloße Körperempfindung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Experiencing emotions as bodily sensations with the cognitive element stilled, giving a state usable for magical work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Emotional Engineering, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnDeconstructingEmotionalState a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Deconstructing an emotional state"@en, "Dekonstruktion eines Gefühlszustands"@de ;
    skos:definition "a) In a powerful emotion, do not suppress it but let yourself be absorbed; b) notice bodily sensations as it intensifies; c) by will, still the inner dialogue's cycling through past causes and projected futures; d) stay with the sensation and keep stilling thoughts that label it as a particular emotion; e) use the resulting state for magic, e.g. entering Liminal Gnosis."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondCEmotionalEngineering (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 practice begins by visualising a worst-case demon situation and ends in an unlabelled sensation; here the same four steps are given as the simplest method, without the visualisation, and a fifth is added: the resulting state is used for magic such as entering the Liminal Gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Emotional Engineering, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEmotionalDeconstructionYieldsUsableState a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Emotional deconstruction yields usable state / Liminal Gnosis"@en, "Gefühlsdekonstruktion führt zu Liminaler Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "The resulting state of awareness can be used for magic such as entry into Liminal Gnosis."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnDeconstructingEmotionalState ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEmotionAsBodilySensation, pmc:ChaosMagicPsnLiminalGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Emotional Engineering, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingQuasiHumanForm a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting into quasi-human or beast form"@en, "Gestaltwandel in quasi-menschliche Form"@de ;
    skos:definition "Transition from human to quasi-human form (e.g. Deep One); useful in astral exploration and in the Becoming the Beast process."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Shape-Shifting, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingModesYieldShapeShift a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Meditation and excitatory trance yield shape-shifting"@en, "Meditation und Trance führen zum Gestaltwandel"@de ;
    skos:definition "Either meditation or excitatory trance can bring about the shape-shift."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingByMeditation, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingByExcitatoryTrance ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingQuasiHumanForm ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Shape-Shifting, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingByMeditation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting by meditation"@en, "Gestaltwandel durch Meditation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Visualise one's body outline changing while using kinaesthetic memory to feel a shift of posture and of centre of gravity."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCAstralShapeShifting (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 practice takes on zoomorphic forms on the astral to enter astral spaces; here the meditative mode is described: the body outline is visualised changing while kinaesthetic memory feels the shift of posture and centre of gravity."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Shape-Shifting, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingByExcitatoryTrance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting by excitatory trance"@en, "Gestaltwandel durch erregende Trance"@de ;
    skos:definition "A trance taking the form of violent possession by the required zoomorphic shape, preceded by excitatory techniques."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCRitualShapeShiftingAnimalPossession (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 practice draws an animal form on with masks, dance and costume in ritual; here the excitatory mode is a violent possession by the required zoomorphic shape, preceded by seething, wild dancing or group ritual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Shape-Shifting, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnExcitatoryPreparationShapeShiftPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Excitatory preparation before shape-shift possession"@en, "Erregende Vorbereitung vor Besessenheits-Gestaltwandel"@de ;
    skos:definition "The excitatory shape-shifting trance should be preceded by seething, wild dancing or group ritual."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnExcitatoryPreliminaries ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnShapeShiftingByExcitatoryTrance ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Shape-Shifting, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnExcitatoryPreliminaries a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Excitatory preliminaries: wild dancing, seething, group ritual"@en, "Erregende Vorbereitung: wilder Tanz, Seething"@de ;
    skos:definition "Raise excitatory trance through seething, wild dancing or group ritual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Shape-Shifting, p. 29; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGlossolaliaWithIntent a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Glossolalia with prior intent"@en, "Glossolalie mit vorheriger Absicht"@de ;
    skos:definition "Rapidly deliver random vowel-consonant sets; preface the exercise with a particular intention so that power-words for that purpose develop."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Glossolalia, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGlossolaliaYieldsGnosisAndPowerWords a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Glossolalia yields gnosis/possession and power-words"@en, "Glossolalie führt zu Gnosis und Machtworten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Glossolalia can develop a route to gnosis ending in possession, and with intent produces power-words; Mythos glossolalia yields mantras into the Old Ones' gnosis."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGlossolaliaWithIntent, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnMythosGlossolalia ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPossessionByNamelessMasks, pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPowerWordsPrimalMantras ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Glossolalia, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPossessionByNamelessMasks a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Possession by nameless masks speaking in word-bursts"@en, "Besessenheit durch namenlose Masken"@de ;
    skos:definition "A route to gnosis climaxing in possession by nameless masks that speak in bursts of words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Glossolalia, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPowerWordsPrimalMantras a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Personal power-words and primal mantras"@en, "Eigene Machtworte und Urmantras"@de ;
    skos:definition "Self-developed words of power for varied uses; primal mantras that open routes into Old-One gnosis."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Glossolalia, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnMythosGlossolalia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Mythos glossolalia: melding with a facet of the Old Ones"@en, "Mythos-Glossolalie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Enter trance by any preferred method; merge your awareness with one particular aspect of the Old Ones, aiming at what lies beyond the name; as perception shifts start glossolalia and keep on until a burst of sound develops; vibrate the emerging mantras variously, from staccato hissing to deep stomach rumble; experiment."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCBarbarousNamesCthulhuoidLanguages (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node reports that some magicians craft Cthulhuoid languages from the barbarous names of the Mythos; here sound-bursts are drawn out of trance by glossolalia while melding with a facet of the Old Ones beyond its name."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Glossolalia, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEnchantmentSecondaryInMythos a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Enchantment of secondary importance in Mythos magick"@en, "Verzauberung zweitrangig in Mythos-Magie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Enchantment techniques aimed at direct results are not primary in Mythos magick, since the Old Ones care little for human desires; it is less sorcerous than other paradigms but not devoid of sorcery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sorcery, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFetishImagesTotemsWeapons a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Making fetish images, totems and weapons"@en, "Herstellung von Fetischbildern, Totems, Waffen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Make fetish images, totems and ritual weapons to surround oneself with physical tokens of the alchemical identification with the outer voids."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sorcery, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnRlyehTransmissionPoint a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "R'Lyeh as transmission point for sigils"@en, "R'Lyeh als Sendepunkt für Sigillen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Treat R'Lyeh as a point of transmission to broadcast sigilised desires outward like a telepathic ripple."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sorcery, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTelepathicBroadcastOfSigil a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Telepathic broadcast of sigilised desire"@en, "Telepathische Aussendung sigillierter Wünsche"@de ;
    skos:definition "Broadcasting sigilised desires like a telepathic ripple (enchantment)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sorcery, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPossessionByTsathoggua a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Partial possession by Tsathoggua"@en, "Teilweise Besessenheit durch Tsathoggua"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author once was possessed by Tsathoggua in the course of a healing ceremony."@en ;
    skos:note "Mentioned under Sorcery and told at length in the appendix Old Toad Under A Mountain, where the possession is called partial."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sorcery, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCreatingObsessiveFetishes a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Creating and collecting obsessive fetishes"@en, "Herstellen und Sammeln obsessiver Fetische"@de ;
    skos:definition "Make or collect personal fetishes, artworks and devices; any object that comes to be linked with a resonant mental state qualifies, not only a clay Cthulhu."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Obsessive Fetishes, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFetishesYieldEmbeddedAwareness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Fetishes aid embedding Mythos awareness"@en, "Fetische fördern das Mythos-Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Creating and collecting personal fetishes aids the embedding of Mythos awareness."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCEmbeddingCthulhuMythos (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 practice enters the Mythos through its tales and makes images of the resulting visions, which can later serve as fetishes; here making and collecting fetishes, any object tied to a resonant state, is itself said to aid the embedding."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCreatingObsessiveFetishes ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEmbeddedMythosAwareness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Obsessive Fetishes, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEmbeddedMythosAwareness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Embedded Mythos awareness"@en, "Verankertes Mythos-Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mythos awareness embedded in daily life; images and objects conveying experiences not easily put into words."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Obsessive Fetishes, p. 30; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPendulumGlassFearSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heralds of the Old Ones' nearness: pendulum, glass-fear"@en, "Vorboten der Nähe der Alten"@de ;
    skos:definition "For the author, stirrings of a crystal pendulum on a retort stand came to herald onset of a liminal gnosis and the sense of the Old Ones' nearness, together with rising fear of window-panes and global significance in everyday sounds such as a ticking clock."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of the nearness of the Old Ones and the beginning of a liminal gnosis, heralded for Hine by the stirrings of a pendulum, a rising fear of window-panes and a global significance in everyday sounds. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline or rate; the sign belongs to one phase of his own work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Obsessive Fetishes, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOracularPerception a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Oracular perception"@en, "Orakelhafte Wahrnehmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being swept into oracular time, distinct from deliberate divination: meaning read from environmental events such as wheeling starlings, traffic movement or a creaking gate; dependent on the magician's state (see Purposive Disintegration)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Obsessive Fetishes, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFrenziedRites a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Frenzied rites: physical gnosis in communion"@en, "Rasende Riten: körperliche Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Use drumming, chanting, flagellation, dancing and abandonment of sexual taboos and restraint, as in Bacchanalia or the witches' sabbat, to celebrate the Old Ones' primal power."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCFrenziedRitesPowerSpots (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node reads Lovecraft's frenzied rites as showing how belief, landscape and the Old Ones interpenetrate; here such rites are offered for use as communion in which the barrier between order and chaos dissolve and the self is negated."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Frenzy, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFrenzyYieldsEcstaticCommunion a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Frenzy yields ecstatic communion"@en, "Raserei führt zu ekstatischer Kommunion"@de ;
    skos:definition "Frenzied cultic activity can be used as communion in which self is negated."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFrenziedRites, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGroupFreeAreasOfAbandonment ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEcstaticCommunion ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Frenzy, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEcstaticCommunion a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Ecstatic communion dissolving self and order/chaos walls"@en, "Ekstatische Kommunion"@de ;
    skos:definition "Communion in which the barrier between order and chaos dissolve momentarily and selfhood is negated within ecstatic frenzy."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Frenzy, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAbandonmentHardInWesternGroupsFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Total abandonment hard in western group ritual"@en, "Völlige Hingabe in westlichen Gruppen schwer"@de ;
    skos:definition "Total abandonment is extremely difficult in western magick, especially in groups, where spontaneity clashes with linear ritual sequencing; the area needs more work."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of total abandonment in western group ritual, where spontaneity clashes with the step-by-step order of most rites. No deadline; the rate words are extremely difficult and rare; the cause is put on the ritual form and the group setting, and the area is said to need more work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Frenzy, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGroupFreeAreasOfAbandonment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Building group \"Free Areas\" of abandonment"@en, "Gruppen-\"Freie Zonen\" der Hingabe"@de ;
    skos:definition "Combine masks, whirling, slow strobe lighting, taped sound effects and sudden changes of tempo in the ritual; heighten unpredictability by not telling all celebrants what to expect; judicious mood-altering chemognosis may help."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Frenzy, p. 31; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSexualArousalSpringboardAstral a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual arousal as springboard to astral/dream zones"@en, "Sexuelle Erregung als Sprungbrett"@de ;
    skos:definition "Use altered states brought on by sexual arousal as a springboard to explore astral or dream zones (specialised Mythos sex magick found in Kenneth Grant)."@en ;
    skos:note "Specialised Mythos sexual techniques are referred to Kenneth Grant's writings and not described."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sexual Magick, p. 32; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSexualGnosisChargeFetishesBreakTaboos a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Charging fetishes and breaking taboos with sexual gnosis"@en, "Fetische laden und Tabus brechen mit Sexualgnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Charge obsessive fetishes with sexual gnosis, and explore sexual gnosis outside one's usual references to break down taboos and revulsions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sexual Magick, p. 32; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOrchestratedOrgiesFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Orchestrated orgies rarely succeed"@en, "Inszenierte Orgien gelingen selten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Deliberately orchestrated orgiastic rites seldom succeed, because success depends on those present being able to abandon themselves."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of deliberately orchestrated orgiastic rites, which rarely succeed. No deadline; the rate word is rarely; the cause is put on the participants' ability to abandon themselves."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sexual Magick, p. 32; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSlowArousalRestraintSeership a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Slow arousal with restraint for liminal seership"@en, "Langsame Erregung mit Zurückhaltung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Combine slow sexual arousal and restraint to bring a subject to Liminal Gnosis so that the subject can enter astral regions and return with information for the other celebrants."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sexual Magick, p. 32; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSexualGnosisYieldsAstralEntry a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual gnosis yields astral exploration"@en, "Sexualgnosis führt zur Astralerkundung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Arousal-induced states, and slow arousal with restraint, allow entry to astral zones."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSexualArousalSpringboardAstral, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSlowArousalRestraintSeership ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamAstralJourneysMythos, pmc:ChaosMagicPsnLiminalGnosis ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sexual Magick, p. 32; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSexualTechniquesExperienceTrustCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Sexual techniques need experience and trust"@en, "Sexualtechniken erfordern Erfahrung und Vertrauen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Such sexual techniques require considerable experience and trust from all concerned."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSexualArousalSpringboardAstral, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSlowArousalRestraintSeership, pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSexualGnosisChargeFetishesBreakTaboos ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Mythos and Magick, Sexual Magick, p. 32; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# The Great Old Ones
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGreatOldOnesForgottenEverPresent a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Great Old Ones: forgotten, ever-present, unbindable"@en, "Die Großen Alten: vergessen, allgegenwärtig"@de ;
    skos:definition "Titanic powers of creation and destruction cast out and forgotten by civilised humanity, yet ever-present at the frontiers of order where wild nature is felt; chaotic like Nature; powerful because they cannot be explained (bound) or anthropomorphised; outside linear time."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCGreatOldOnesNaming (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node places the Old Ones outside human mythologies, too chaotic for orthodox systems, with fear of the unknown attached; here they are titanic powers of creation and destruction cast out and forgotten by civilised humanity, ever-present at the frontiers of order, chaotic as Nature is and outside linear time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, p. 33; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesFractalSurface a pm:Systematizing ;
    rdfs:label "Old Ones as one fractal surface (\"Nature's Chaos\")"@en, "Die Alten als eine fraktale Oberfläche"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's model: Azathoth, Yog-Sothoth and Nyarlathotep treated as parameters of one conterminous entity; the Old Ones as a seething fractal surface whose distinct shapes arise from our interaction with them, called Nature's Chaos; separating them is useful mainly for forming distinct practices and cults."@en ;
    skos:note "Author-made scheme (pm:Systematizing): Hine's own model of the Old Ones for this book, not an order the Mythos tales give."@en ;
    pm:compiledBy "Phil Hine, in The Pseudonomicon" ;
    dcterms:date "2004"^^xsd:gYear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, p. 33; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOldOnesInWildPlacesAndWithin a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Presence in wild places and within; aspects await discovery"@en, "Gegenwart an wilden Orten und innen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Where the chaos of nature outweighs the rules of society, in wild places, the Old Ones can perhaps be perceived (as Greeks held frontier places sacred to wild gods bringing terror or transfiguration); their presence can also be felt within through states of willed madness. Further aspects await personal discovery."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine leaves further aspects of the Old Ones to personal discovery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, p. 33; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnYogSothothNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Yog-Sothoth"@en, "Yog-Sothoth"@de ;
    skos:definition "The gate, its key and its guardian in whom past, present and future are one (after The Dunwich Horror). Once seen by the author as a guide entity into states for Old-One work (Earth Lights research); since workings with Fra. Abbadon (ex-Esoteric Order of Dagon) understood as the outer edge (mathematical basis) of the fractal surface, an experience the magician slips into by degrees."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCAzathothYogSothothNaming (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives Azathoth and Yog-Sothoth together as utterly alien Old Ones; here Yog-Sothoth is the gate, once taken by Hine as a guide entity and now as the outer edge of the fractal surface into which the magician slips."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Yog-Sothoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "gate; outer edge of the Old Ones' experience" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 34; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEncounterPhenomenaCommonFeatures a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Common features of encounter phenomena"@en, "Gemeinsame Merkmale von Begegnungsphänomenen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Researching ghosts, UFOs and light-forms (Paul Devereux's Earth Lights hypothesis), the author found strange sounds, distorted time-sense, strong smells and strange visions common across encounters."@en ;
    skos:note "Framed by Paul Devereux's Earth Lights hypothesis, named without a work; only the finding is entered."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 34; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearingTheBeast a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Fearing the Beast"@en, "Das Tier fürchten"@de ;
    skos:definition "First stage of Becoming the Beast: fear as the reaction of the magician's sense of his own boundaries, the ego before antinomian deconstruction; more than a guard against the outer spaces, for fear purged of attachment and felt as bare bodily sensation becomes an excitatory gnosis that leads on."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeelingTheBeastSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Signs of Feeling the Beast"@en, "Zeichen des Fühlens des Tieres"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fear freed of attachment and sunk into, like the Hanged Man, turns into an altered state; Feeling the Beast shows itself through sensuous gnosis of the primitive brain: strange kinaesthetic manifestations, astral sounds, sense of nearness of something vast and shadowy; it is recognition of bodily gnosis and mental images indicating immersion in the Old Ones' realm."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of immersion in the Old Ones' realm at the stage called Feeling the Beast: odd kinaesthetic effects, sounds on the astral and a sense of nearness to something vast and shadowy. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline or rate."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeelingTheBeast a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Feeling the Beast"@en, "Das Tier fühlen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Second stage of Becoming the Beast: a sensuous gnosis rooted in the brain's older, more primitive regions, in which the magician recognises both the bodily gnosis and the higher-order mental images that show immersion in the Old Ones' realm."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeelBeforeFeedPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Fear, then Feel, then Feed (Feast)"@en, "Fürchten, dann Fühlen, dann Nähren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Feast of Yog-Sothoth is the final stage, coming after fearing and feeling the Beast; the excitatory gnosis of purged fear leads into Feeling, which leads into Feeding."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeelingTheBeast ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFeastOfYogSothoth ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBecomingTheBeastStages a pm:Sequencing ;
    rdfs:label "Becoming the Beast: Fear, Feel, Feed"@en, "Zum Tier werden: Fürchten, Fühlen, Füttern"@de ;
    skos:definition "The three stages of integrating the Monstrous Souls as Hine names and orders them: fearing the Beast, feeling it, and feeding it, the last enacted in the Feast of Yog-Sothoth."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en, "The stages are named under Becoming the Beast and worked out under Yog-Sothoth; no consequence of skipping a stage is named."@en ;
    rdf:_1 pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearingTheBeast ;
    rdf:_2 pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeelingTheBeast ;
    rdf:_3 pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFeastOfYogSothoth ;
    pm:seriesStatement "Hine names the three stages in this order, and the third has its sense only after the first two: fear purged of attachment turns into the excitatory gnosis by which the Beast is felt, and only one who has recognised immersion in the Old Ones' realm can be eaten by the experience in the Feast of Yog-Sothoth." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35 (stages named under Invocations of Otherness, Becoming the Beast, p. 17); read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFeastOfYogSothoth a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Feast of Yog-Sothoth"@en, "Das Fest des Yog-Sothoth"@de ;
    skos:definition "Final stage of the Beast process (Feed the Beast): deliberately let oneself be eaten by the experience, the veil of fear against otherness being lost."@en ;
    skos:note "Likened to the Babalon mysteries of Thelema, to Kali's feast in the vama marg form of Tantra, and to Choronzon as Kenneth Grant presents the Typhonian Current."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFeastYieldsFullConsciousness a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Feast of Yog-Sothoth yields full Old-One consciousness"@en, "Fest des Yog-Sothoth führt zu vollem Bewusstsein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being eaten by the experience reaches complete awareness of the Old Ones."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFeastOfYogSothoth ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFullConsciousnessOfOldOnes ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFullConsciousnessOfOldOnes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Complete awareness of the Old Ones; Self-Love, supersensuality; becoming a gateway"@en, "Volles Bewusstsein der Alten; Torwerdung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Complete awareness of the Old Ones, with Self-Love and Spare's supersensuality as by-products; having melded with the outer edge, the magician himself becomes a gateway."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Yog-Sothoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAzathothNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Azathoth"@en, "Azathoth"@de ;
    skos:definition "Blind idiot god, monstrous nuclear chaos, lord of all things, mindlessly producing chaotic forms; in a possession working (Mass of Chaos \"A\", Liber Kaos) described as having a skin of mad souls; claimed to project and gather obsessional thought-forms; perhaps Logoic, churning the fractal surface; the whirling void appearing as the ego-persona is deconstructed; the eye of Chaos whose blindness recalls retroversion of the senses."@en ;
    skos:note "The skin of mad souls is reported from a possession working (the Mass of Chaos \"A\", referred to Carroll's Liber Kaos) and the gathering of obsessional thought-forms as a claim of unnamed others; the reading as churning, void and eye is Hine's."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCAzathothYogSothothNaming (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives Azathoth only as an utterly alien Old One beside Yog-Sothoth; here it is the churning of the fractal surface, the whirling void of the deconstructed ego and the eye of Chaos."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Azathoth" ;
    pm:nameRole "churning chaos; whirling void of deconstruction" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Azathoth, p. 35; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPersonaIdentificationMegalomaniaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Persona identification leading to megalomania"@en, "Persona-Identifikation führt zu Größenwahn"@de ;
    skos:definition "Azathoth recalls the danger of identifying with one persona over another: obsession that may rapidly become megalomania (Crowley's Black Brothers)."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAzathothNaming ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Azathoth, p. 36; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnUnheededManiaWarningsFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Unheeded mania warnings end in disintegration"@en, "Missachtete Manie-Warnungen enden im Zerfall"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mania from magical work at any stage is well documented; those who ignore warnings usually slide into disintegration while straining to keep an ego-image at odds with their real existence."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of magicians who ignore warnings about mania, who usually slide into disintegration while keeping up an ego-image at odds with the facts of their lives. No deadline; the rate word is usually; the cause is put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Azathoth, p. 36; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAzathothTrialFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Azathoth trial: becoming bound to one thing"@en, "Azathoth-Prüfung: an eines gebunden werden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Azathoth holds an initiatory test: not to get bound up in any single thing; failing it, one suffers the lot of the mad souls forming its skin."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of the initiatory trial Hine places in Azathoth, failed by getting bound up in any single thing, the penalty being the lot of the mad souls of its skin. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on the operator's attachment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Azathoth, p. 36; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNyarlathotepNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Nyarlathotep"@en, "Nyarlathotep"@de ;
    skos:definition "Marked out by seeming intelligent and interested in human affairs; identified with the black man of the sabbat, Choronzon-Shugal the howling guardian, resembling Crowley's Aiwass; crawling chaos and messenger and soul of the Old Ones; initiator, nemesis and source of confusion; presides over the astral sabbat and guards dream-gates like Leng's Monastery; the gateway to Old-One gnosis."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCNyarlathotepCrawlingChaosNaming (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives Nyarlathotep as the one intelligent Old One and leader of the sabbat; here it is also initiator, nemesis and source of confusion, the gateway into Old-One gnosis, and the stage of becoming aware of one's self-erected boundaries."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Nyarlathotep" ;
    pm:nameRole "messenger; initiator; gateway to the gnosis" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, pp. 36-37; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNyarlathotepPossessionMisleadsFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Invoking Nyarlathotep by possession misleads"@en, "Nyarlathotep-Anrufung durch Besessenheit täuscht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author's experiments invoking Nyarlathotep through rites of possession only confirmed its power to mislead and obfuscate; it proved as twisted and cunning as a Goetic demon."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of Hine's experiments invoking Nyarlathotep by rites of possession, which only confirmed its power to mislead and obscure. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on the entity and the choice of method, and he turned to dreaming instead."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 37; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnNyarlathotepDreamContact a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Contacting Nyarlathotep through dreaming"@en, "Kontakt mit Nyarlathotep durch Träumen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seek contact with Nyarlathotep in dream, where he rules the astral sabbat or watches over dream-gates like Leng's Monastery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 37; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGnosisOfTheOldOnes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Old-One gnosis"@en, "Gnosis der Großen Alten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Knowledge of the Old Ones, attainable through dream or delirium or arising unbidden in the naturally resonant; Nyarlathotep is its gateway, the stage of becoming aware, often painfully, of the limits one has set to one's Achievable Reality."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 37; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamContactYieldsGnosis a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Dream contact yields Old-One gnosis"@en, "Traumkontakt führt zur Gnosis"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dream contact with Nyarlathotep opens Old-One gnosis, attainable through dream or delirium."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnNyarlathotepDreamContact ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGnosisOfTheOldOnes ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 37; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicianSelfAvatarNyarlathotep a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magician-self: avatar of Nyarlathotep, completed Adept as gateway"@en, "Magier-Selbst: Avatar Nyarlathoteps"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician-self of Carroll's Octarine Gnosis (antinomian, devious, interested in what should not exist, nothing unnatural to it): an invocation of future otherness by which the completed Adept becomes a gateway and avatar of Nyarlathotep."@en ;
    skos:note "Carroll's account of the magician-self in Liber Kaos (the Octarine Gnosis) is quoted for its characteristics; the node holds Hine's use of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 38; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDeconstructionYieldsAvatarship a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Deconstruction yields avatarship of Nyarlathotep"@en, "Dekonstruktion führt zur Avatarschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "Through continual practice of deconstruction the magician turns into an avatar of Nyarlathotep."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnContinualDeconstructionOfBoundaries ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMagicianSelfAvatarNyarlathotep ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 38; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnImageIdentificationFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Identification with an image of Nyarlathotep fails"@en, "Identifikation mit einem Bild Nyarlathoteps versagt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mere identification with a mental picture of Nyarlathotep does not achieve the magician-self, any more than obsessing over Crowley makes one the Beast 666."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of mere identification with a mental image of Nyarlathotep, which does not reach the magician-self, any more than obsession with Crowley makes one the Beast 666. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on the method."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 38; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnContinualDeconstructionOfBoundaries a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Continual deconstruction of boundaries; reducing oneself to a void"@en, "Ständige Grenzauflösung; sich zur Leere machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Through continual practice strip away all barriers and boundaries; Crowley's technique of reducing oneself to a void so the genius plays through as it will."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Nyarlathotep, p. 38; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGreatCthulhuNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Great Cthulhu (as the text interprets)"@en, "Großer Cthulhu (Deutung)"@de ;
    skos:definition "High Priest and cousin of the Old Ones, in death's dream in R'Lyeh until the stars are right; stirrings send telepathic ripples (visions, nightmares, breakdowns, dream-inspired art); Lord of Dreams; analogous to chaotic perceptions and desires sealed in the subconscious; a buried link in human memory to the Old Ones' gnosis; a gateway under the ocean (Pan's domain) and an access-point to beast-consciousness."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCCthulhuNaming (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives Lovecraft's Cthulhu, lord of dreams and intermediary whose transmissions bring insanity; here Cthulhu is read as the chaotic perceptions and desires sealed in the subconscious, a buried link in memory to Old-One gnosis, an initiatory gateway under the ocean and an access-point to beast-consciousness."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Great Cthulhu" ;
    pm:nameRole "High Priest; Lord of Dreams" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Great Cthulhu, pp. 38-39; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnHeardTheCall a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Heard the call: summons to initiation draws one from consensus"@en, "Den Ruf hören: Initiationsruf"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cthulhu and R'Lyeh send currents into humanity; whoever has heard its call is drawn inexorably into the depths, revelation drawing the aspiring magician out of consensus reality; deep ocean as chaos/underworld; R'Lyeh's sinking as a variant of the Fall."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Great Cthulhu, p. 38; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBeastConsciousnessAtavisticResurgence a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Beast-consciousness via Atavistic Resurgence"@en, "Tierbewusstsein durch atavistische Wiederkehr"@de ;
    skos:definition "Access to the lost participation mystique of zoomorphic god-forms (Set, Medusa, Typhon, Hanuman), Cthulhu being an access-point through Spare's Atavistic Resurgence."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Great Cthulhu, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRlyehExtrusionOfCthulhu a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "R'Lyeh as extrusion of Cthulhu; astral geometry like the Tunnels of Set"@en, "R'Lyeh als Ausstülpung Cthulhus"@de ;
    skos:definition "R'Lyeh is extruded by Cthulhu as a spider's web; explored astrally its geometry shifts so tunnels collapse and overlap, like the Tunnels of Set; its paths are recurrently felt to link the stars with the dark earth."@en ;
    skos:note "The shifting geometry and the paths linking earth and stars are reported as recurring images from the astral explorations of unnamed practitioners."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCRLyehPsychicGateway (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node gives R'Lyeh as a city of gates comparable to the Tunnels of Set and a gateway to the Deep Mind; here R'Lyeh is extruded by Cthulhu as a spider extrudes a web, its astral geometry shifts and collapses, and it is itself taken as a form of the Tunnels of Set."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Great Cthulhu, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnShubNiggurathNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Shub-Niggurath"@en, "Shub-Niggurath"@de ;
    skos:definition "The wood's black goat with its thousand young, never met directly but hinted at in spell-fragments; suggested to be a primal form of Pan; possibly the terror of wild places, embodying fecundity and tangled growth, treatable as the swollen spirit of place of any wilderness, a growth from the fleeting images of a lonely night there."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Shub-Niggurath" ;
    pm:nameRole "bloated genius loci of wild places" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Shub-Niggurath, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnRapportWithWildPlaceSpirits a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeking rapport with spirits of wild places"@en, "Rapport mit Geistern wilder Orte suchen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spend lonely nights in wild places, seek out the local spirits under their own forms and names, and if possible enter rapport with them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Shub-Niggurath, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRapportWithGeniusLoci a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rapport with wild-place spirits"@en, "Rapport mit Geistern wilder Orte"@de ;
    skos:definition "A working relationship with the genius loci of a wild place."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), The Great Old Ones, Shub-Niggurath, p. 39; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Transfigurations
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTransfigurationAsTransgressiveInitiation a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Transfiguration as transgressive initiation"@en, "Transfiguration als transgressive Initiation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Mythos transformations (Deep Ones, Ghouls) come from dark ancestry or affinity and are implied to follow directly from entering the Old Ones' realm; they echo world-wide human-beast initiation myths with transgression at their core (Lykaon; Gwydion and Gilfaethwy). In the Becoming the Beast process the magician dismantles ego boundaries, steps back from conditioning and acknowledges atavistic desires society calls evil or animal."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnMonstrousShapeShiftingRlyeh a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Shape-shifting into monstrous forms to explore R'Lyeh"@en, "Gestaltwandel in Monsterformen zur Erkundung R'Lyehs"@de ;
    skos:definition "Use shape-shifting into monstrous forms as a technique to explore astral spaces such as R'Lyeh's bizarre geometry or other dreamlands, or for sensory and kinaesthetic experience induced by trance."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnFeastOfTheDemonsDismemberment a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Ritual feint of psychic dismemberment (Feast of the Demons)"@en, "Rituelle psychische Zerstückelung"@de ;
    skos:definition "As in the Tibetan rite called the Feast of the Demons, the celebrant offers himself to his own demonic complexes, to be torn apart by them so as to be reborn; such experiences may also occur spontaneously in initiatory crisis."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCDismemberment (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node surveys dismemberment myths and names Chod as a voluntary meditation; here the Tibetan rite called the Feast of the Demons is a ritual feint at psychic dismemberment, one level of practical transfiguration work, with the Ghoul's Feast as a variant of it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, p. 41; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRebirthThroughDismemberment a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rebirth through dismemberment"@en, "Wiedergeburt durch Zerstückelung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being re-born after symbolic dismemberment by one's own complexes."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGhoulsFeast a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "The Ghoul's Feast"@en, "Das Ghulfest"@de ;
    skos:definition "Spend a night alone in wilderness offering one's body as sacrifice to the Ghouls; parts of the body may be smeared with animal offal; no circle or trappings, the stance being total helplessness. The rite may be partly astral, or other celebrants play the ghouls, dismembering the celebrant's self-importance with mockery. Climax: passage from human to the company of ghouls, marked by rolling, or being rolled, through mud or excrement, sharing offal (especially brain), or copulating with a celebrant made up as a corpse; chemognosis may intensify it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, The Ghoul's Feast, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGhoulsFeastYieldsPassage a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Ghoul's Feast yields ghoul-passage"@en, "Ghulfest führt zum Ghul-Übergang"@de ;
    skos:definition "The rite appears to climax in transformation of the celebrant into one of the ghouls."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGhoulsFeast ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPassageIntoCompanyOfGhouls ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, The Ghoul's Feast, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPassageIntoCompanyOfGhouls a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Passage into the company of ghouls; ego consumed, taboos faced"@en, "Übergang zu den Ghulen; Ego verzehrt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Transformation of status from human to ghoul; consumption of the ego-complex; relinquishing control and facing one's taboos so they cannot be evaded; deliberate severing of beliefs held until then; the ghoul as magician who rejects no part of experience and is attached to nothing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, The Ghoul's Feast, p. 42; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSelfModificationThroughFear a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Self-modification through relaxing into fear"@en, "Selbstveränderung durch Entspannung in die Angst"@de ;
    skos:definition "Relaxing into fear is said to allow self-modification: fear becomes a strength rather than a weakness, the magician stays vulnerable to change and surprise, and the process can turn ecstatic."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCThresholdMomentum (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node is excitement used as fuel for moving into change; here Hine calls what relaxing into fear permits self-modification, with fear as a strength and the process able to become ecstatic."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Getting the Fear, p. 43; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRelaxIntoFearYieldsSelfModification a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Relaxing into fear yields self-modification"@en, "Entspannung in die Angst führt zur Selbstveränderung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Relaxing into fear is said to permit self-modification and can turn ecstatic."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCRelaxIntoFearYieldsOpenness (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 yielding is openness to wild magic and surprise; here relaxing into fear is said to permit self-modification."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicCondCRelaxIntoFear ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSelfModificationThroughFear ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRigidityFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Rigidity impedes new experience"@en, "Starrheit verhindert neue Erfahrung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Holding oneself rigid impedes entering new experiences."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of relaxing into fear and entering new experience, impeded by holding oneself rigid. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on the operator."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnWilledMythicEnactmentFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Willed mythic enactment is not authentic initiation"@en, "Gewollte Mythen-Inszenierung ist keine echte Initiation"@de ;
    skos:definition "Enacting a mythic initiation sequence is not the same as undergoing an authentic trial; myths are only signposts; a willed ritual dismemberment may be less powerful than one lived as crisis."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of a willed enactment of mythic initiation taken for the thing itself; a willed ritual dismemberment may be less powerful than one lived as crisis. No deadline or rate; the cause is put on timing and the celebrant's inner state, not on the form of the rite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnWorkingACrossroads a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Recognising and working a crossroads"@en, "Eine Wegkreuzung erkennen und bearbeiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Recognise that a significant crisis-point is arriving rather than forcing one; then do magical work to make the most of the change the crossroads holds."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCThresholdRecognition (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node makes recognising a threshold the key to initiation; here recognising a crisis-point is set above trying to force one, and magical work is then done to make the most of the change it holds."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMaximisedChangeAtCrossroads a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Maximised change at a life crossroads"@en, "Maximierter Wandel an einer Wegkreuzung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Using a crisis-point for maximal change."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnCrossroadsBeforeGhoulsFeastPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Ghoul's Feast needs the right time and inner state"@en, "Ghulfest braucht rechte Zeit und inneren Zustand"@de ;
    skos:definition "The key to such workings is when, not how or where; the success of initiations like the Ghoul's Feast depends greatly on the celebrant's inner state, i.e. a recognised crossroads."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnWorkingACrossroads ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGhoulsFeast ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "A willed enactment without real crisis may be much less powerful; success depends on the inner state."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTabooExposureConsequencesCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Taboo-exposure workings have immense consequences"@en, "Tabu-Arbeiten haben enorme Folgen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fear and desire mixed give strong intrapsychic reactions, and any working that heightens them through exposure to taboo can have immensely powerful consequences, chief among them a deliberate severing of beliefs held until then and attitudes."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnGhoulsFeast ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 44; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGhoulAsUnattachedMagician a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The Ghoul as image of the unattached magician"@en, "Der Ghul als Bild des ungebundenen Magiers"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Ghoul that stalks by night eating corpses and offal is read as the figure of a magician who rejects no part of his experience any longer and seeks gnosis in every form while attached to nothing; Hine sets beside it Carroll's maxim from Liber Null that the strongest minds hold the fewest fixed principles."@en ;
    skos:note "Carroll's maxim from Liber Null is quoted beside the image; the node holds Hine's reading of the Ghoul."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Transfigurations, Relax into Fear, p. 45; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Purposive Disintegration
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPurposiveDisintegration a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Purposive disintegration: deliberate incursions into borderline mania"@en, "Zielgerichteter Zerfall"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prolonged work with the Old Ones imbues states of awareness that one slips into during communion; the author calls them deliberate incursions into borderline mania."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 46; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFearRefusalAndManiaCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Fear-refusal and mania during the process"@en, "Angstabwehr und Manie im Prozess"@de ;
    skos:definition "The magician may struggle with fear and refusing the unavoidable change and what follows from his flight to consensus reality's edge; gnosis-induced mania is also common."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPurposiveDisintegration ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 46; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAuraOfOnsetSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Aura of onset: signs of tipping into new awareness"@en, "Aura des Beginns"@de ;
    skos:definition "With time one learns to recognise the signs of tipping into new awareness: odd bodily sensations and perceptions and distorted ideas of identity, which belong to the course and may be misread as psychic attack or energy moving into chakras."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of tipping into a new awareness, learned in time: odd bodily sensations and perceptions and distorted ideas of identity, which may be misread as psychic attack or as energy moving into chakras. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline; the only measure of time is in time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 46; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnOracularAwarenessOfChange a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Oracular awareness of impending inner change"@en, "Orakelhaftes Gespür für kommende Wandlungen"@de ;
    skos:definition "A finely tuned ability to identify feelings that precede shifts in awareness, sensing what is on the point of breaking in."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 46; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnCreepingDisintegrationSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Signs of creeping disintegration states"@en, "Zeichen schleichender Zerfallszustände"@de ;
    skos:definition "Strange perceptions slip through linear perception, first faintly disturbing then obsessive; odd suspicions gather; everyday objects (furniture, jars, clocks, plants) seem to have their own power and purpose; shapes glimpsed at the corners of the eyes."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of the states that prolonged Mythos work brings on: perceptions slipping through linear awareness, gathering suspicions, everyday objects taking on power and purpose, half-seen shapes at the edge of sight. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline or rate; the same signs mark the danger the section then addresses."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnPresentYetCataloguing a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Surviving disintegration: present yet cataloguing"@en, "Zerfall überleben: anwesend und katalogisierend"@de ;
    skos:definition "When such states creep in, be fully present yet dispassionately catalogue them, like the hero of Cronenberg's The Fly noting his own disintegration; recognise compulsive personal rituals (touching a spot before sleep) as anxiety, i.e. fear of losing control; give up needing control and feel your hands tremble."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSurvivingBorderlineStates a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Survival through borderline states"@en, "Überleben in Grenzzuständen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Coming through disintegration states intact enough to use them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnNeurochemicalAlchemy a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Neurochemical alchemy: fasting, diet change, sleeplessness, solitude"@en, "Neurochemische Alchemie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Use fasting, drastic changes of diet, lack of sleep and solitude to break down linear consciousness."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBreakdownOfLinearConsciousness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Breakdown of linear consciousness"@en, "Zusammenbruch des linearen Bewusstseins"@de ;
    skos:definition "Linear consciousness breaks down, leaving the half-glimpsed and vague; megalomania and fixed self-identification revealed as illusory; sanity and madness turn into irrelevant fictions."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnNeurochemicalAlchemyYieldsBreakdown a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Neurochemical alchemy yields breakdown of linearity"@en, "Neurochemische Alchemie bricht Linearität"@de ;
    skos:definition "Fasting, diet changes, sleeplessness and solitude break down linear consciousness."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnNeurochemicalAlchemy ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnBreakdownOfLinearConsciousness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnReplayingPastMadness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Replaying past madness through cut-ups of personal history"@en, "Frühere Verrücktheit wiederholen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Having been through borderline paranoia, remember it and take on its thoughts and behaviours, almost as cut-ups of personal history, to replay it; it may start as acting, but done well it invokes one's old demons."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnReplayOldDemonsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Replay invokes old demons that take over"@en, "Wiederholung ruft alte Dämonen, die übernehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Done well, replaying madness makes one jerk like a puppet to old demons' urges (facial twitches, jerking hands, rocking, as the author displayed before a roomful of people)."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnReplayingPastMadness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnReplayedMadnessYieldsDirectPerception a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Replayed madness yields direct perception of the Old Ones"@en, "Wiederholte Verrücktheit führt zur Wahrnehmung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Replaying borderline paranoia let the author perceive the Old Ones directly, though communication failed."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnReplayingPastMadness ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDirectPerceptionOfOldOnes ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDirectPerceptionOfOldOnes a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Direct perception of the Old Ones' reality"@en, "Direkte Wahrnehmung der Wirklichkeit der Alten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Gripped by demons called up from his past, the author perceived the Old Ones' reality directly and try (unsuccessfully) to communicate it to others."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 47; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAllMasksEqual a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "After the self shatters, all masks are equal"@en, "Nach dem Zerbrechen des Selbst sind alle Masken gleich"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the bright self shatters, other selves fill the vacuum, but in time all masks (Higher Self, Dark Genius) are equal temporary formulations; under the Night of Pan it may stop but never ends; a time comes when drawing back from the abyss is no longer an option."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCFearLosingControlWorseDerangement (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node says Lovecraft is said to have drawn back from derangement and that fear of losing control holds one back; here all masks become equal once the bright self shatters, and a time is said to come when drawing back is no longer an option."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnDumpingConceptOfMadness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Dumping the concept of madness"@en, "Den Begriff des Wahnsinns aufgeben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Since fear of madness persists only while one holds a coherent picture of madness, drop the concept and open yourself to sensation."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSchizophrenicSpeechInnerPlane a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Schizophrenic speech and inner-plane communications"@en, "Schizophrene Rede und Mitteilungen innerer Ebenen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine says he has seen people diagnosed as schizophrenic try to convey their world-view in a jumble of unconnected phrases and numbers much like some messages said to come from inner-plane adepts; the meaning can almost be grasped, and only from within the fitting gnosis."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEquilibriumForEffectivenessPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Equilibrium needed to be effective"@en, "Gleichgewicht nötig, um wirksam zu sein"@de ;
    skos:definition "Unlike the schizophrenic, who may never regain equilibrium, the magician must regain it at least some of the time in order to act effectively in the world."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSurvivingBorderlineStates ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEffectivenessInTheWorld ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "Without returning to equilibrium the magician is not effective in the world."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEffectivenessInTheWorld a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Effectiveness in the world"@en, "Wirksamkeit in der Welt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Being effective in the world, which Hine requires of the magician at least some of the time, in contrast to the schizophrenic who may never regain equilibrium."@en ;
    skos:note "Added by the integrator from the reading of the whole text; no extraction record carries it."@en, "Written as the dependent step of pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEquilibriumForEffectivenessPrerequisite."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCourtingMadness a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Courting madness and crossing the line repeatedly"@en, "Den Wahnsinn umwerben, die Linie wiederholt überschreiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Court madness now and send forth whatever is left; recross the thin line repeatedly until one makes no distinction between the two states."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRiskingDerangement a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Magic should risk derangement; initiatory sickness"@en, "Magie sollte Verwirrung riskieren"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author sees no point in a magical approach that never risks derangement; unsanity is a major cultural taboo and so worth exploring; such states are known as initiatory sickness, akin to schizophrenia (one may mimic schizophrenic gnosis by reading R.D. Laing)."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCInitiatorySickness (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node gives initiatory sickness as the herald of a summons that tribal cultures treat as useful development; here Hine says he sees no point in a magical approach that never risks derangement, and calls unsanity a taboo worth a look for that alone."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnCourtingMadnessYieldsUnclutteredWill a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Repeated crossing yields uncluttered will"@en, "Wiederholtes Überschreiten führt zu freiem Willen"@de ;
    skos:definition "From the point of no difference between states, will is uncluttered and mad thoughts work for you."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCourtingMadness ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnUnclutteredWill ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnUnclutteredWill a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Will uncluttered; mad thoughts working for you"@en, "Unverstellter Wille; verrückte Gedanken arbeiten für dich"@de ;
    skos:definition "Will freed of restrictive images, so one can release mad thoughts and have them work as one wills."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 48; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDiscardingHumanImperatives a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Discarding good/evil, sanity, Being Right, Getting Even"@en, "Ablegen von Gut/Böse, Recht-Haben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Because human notions of good, evil, sanity and insanity, and drives like Being Right or Getting Even mean nothing to the Old Ones, working with them is said to let one discard these quickly."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Purposive Disintegration, p. 50; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Nightlands
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamingMainRouteDreamland a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dreaming and astral vision as main route; Lovecraft's Dreamland"@en, "Träumen als Hauptweg; Lovecrafts Traumland"@de ;
    skos:definition "Dreaming and astral vision are among the commonest routes into the Old Ones' gnosis; Lovecraft's Dreamland (after Dunsany) touches the physical world and gives access to Leng, Kadath and outer planets; it is built from nostalgia, idealised past, fantasy and fear of what lies at its borders."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, p. 50; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnAstralSabbat a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Entering and working the Astral Sabbat"@en, "Den Astralsabbat betreten und bearbeiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Enter the astral sabbat (presided over by Nyarlathotep, the Man in Black, usually in a wild spot such as a cavern or forest clearing) via dreams or Liminal Gnosis; once contact is made, visit in astral form, human or not, and interact deliberately with its non-human celebrants, acting freely within the dream."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, pp. 50-51; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSpontaneousSabbatDreamsSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Spontaneous dreams of the sabbat as positive indicator"@en, "Spontane Sabbatträume als positives Zeichen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Magicians seeking the Old Ones' mythic reality often dream spontaneously of being at the sabbat; such dreams count as a positive indicator."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of seeking the mythic reality of the Old Ones, in dreams, arriving unbidden, of being at the sabbat, which count as a positive indicator. Judged by the dreamer alone; no deadline; the rate is given only as not unusual."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, p. 50; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSabbatContactYieldsWilledVisits a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Contact yields willed sabbat visits"@en, "Kontakt ermöglicht gewollte Sabbatbesuche"@de ;
    skos:definition "Once contact with the sabbat is made, one can visit it astrally and interact with intent."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnAstralSabbat ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnWilledSabbatInteraction ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, p. 50; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnWilledSabbatInteraction a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Willed interaction at the Astral Sabbat"@en, "Gewollte Interaktion am Astralsabbat"@de ;
    skos:definition "Visiting the sabbat at will and interacting with larval, zoomorphic, succubus/incubus entities."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, p. 50; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSabbatDistractionCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "Distraction and deception at the sabbat"@en, "Ablenkung und Täuschung am Sabbat"@de ;
    skos:definition "Do not be overly distracted by the pleasures elemental entities offer; Qabalists place the sabbat in Yesod, with a degree of fascination and thus deception."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnAstralSabbat ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, p. 51; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnRecordingSabbatVisits a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Recording sabbat visits to test consistency"@en, "Sabbatbesuche aufzeichnen und prüfen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Note down all sabbat experiences and, on later visits, watch for recurring themes and detail that stays consistent over time."@en ;
    skos:note "A check on astral experience by consistency over time."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, p. 51; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnSabbatAsStartingPoint a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sabbat as starting point; establishing work sites in dream"@en, "Sabbat als Ausgangspunkt; Arbeitsorte im Traum"@de ;
    skos:definition "Use the astral sabbat as a launch point for deliberate exploration of the Mythos dreamland; sites you establish as related to your work may then appear in your dreamlands, the waking/dream border often blurring."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, The Astral Sabbat, p. 51; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAstralBooksAndLibrary a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astral books and the astral library"@en, "Astrale Bücher und die astrale Bibliothek"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Necronomicon's power comes from its mythic nature; astral books met in dream (e.g. unwritten books of Crowley) represent gaining information by dream or vision; a recurrent image is an astral library whose books may be sentient; earthing them relates to automatic writing, channelling, and possession by verbose entities."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCAstralBooks (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 node proposes treating the Necronomicon as an astral book personal to its receiver; here the astral library is a recurrent dream image whose books may be sentient, and the power of the Necronomicon is put in its mythic nature."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 51; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnChannelledInformationDegeneratesFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Channelled information degenerates into psychobabble"@en, "Gechannelte Information verkommt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Most such communications reflect the medium's own concerns and beliefs; an initial one may carry much new information, but with increased contact it generally degenerates into psychobabble and obvious assumptions."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of channelled and astral-book communication, which mostly reflects the medium's own beliefs and with time and further contact generally sinks into psychobabble. No deadline; the rate word is generally; the cause is put on the medium."@en, "The spreading of such messages as new truth through vanity presses is already carried by pmc:ChaosMagicPrcCReceiversAstralBooksTurningProphetCautela (Prime Chaos, 1999)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAstralBookRetrievalGestalts a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Astral-book retrieval as meshing gestalts in gnosis; belief-consistency"@en, "Abruf astraler Bücher als Gestalt-Verknüpfung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author views retrieval from astral books as meshing new information-gestalts in gnosis at the edge of one's psychocosm; others may prefer to see it as contact with outer consciousness, dark genius or Holy Guardian Angel. The information tends to agree with the interpreter's belief system (Thelemites find Thelemic data)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnAstralBeachHead a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sigils and dreamscaping to build an astral beach-head"@en, "Sigillen und Traumgestaltung für einen astralen Brückenkopf"@de ;
    skos:definition "Use dreamscaping (described in Prime Chaos), sigils and dream-control techniques to set up an astral beach-head from which to weave the appropriate psychocosm."@en ;
    skos:note "Dreamscaping is referred to Prime Chaos, where it stands as pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCDreamscaping."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamsAndMythosArousal a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Dreams linked to Mythos arousal and borderline states"@en, "Träume an Mythos-Erregung und Grenzzustände gebunden"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine asserts a clear link between dreams and the emotional arousal that the Mythos stirs, above all the borderline states of heightened perception and paranoia."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnDreamControlYieldsNightlands a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Dream techniques yield exploration of nightlands"@en, "Traumtechniken führen zur Erkundung der Nachtlande"@de ;
    skos:definition "With time and practice dream-control techniques make exploration of the Mythos and personal nightlands possible."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnAstralBeachHead ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnExploringNightlands ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnExploringNightlands a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Exploring the Mythos and personal nightlands"@en, "Erkundung des Mythos und eigener Nachtlande"@de ;
    skos:definition "With time and practice, exploring many areas of the Lovecraftian Mythos and one's own nightlands through dream-control."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Nightlands, Astral Books, p. 52; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Dark Zones
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGatewaysInLandscape a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Gateways of the Old Ones in landscape"@en, "Tore der Alten in der Landschaft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Old Ones manifest through gateways, often in wild lonely places tangled with folklore, odd lights, underground noises, ruins and stone circles; also strange angles, tunnels and wells, and the gates of dream, trance and madness. Power spots should carry wildness, even urban ones like mineshafts and abandoned power stations (cf. Keel's Mothman); events add to a site's power; its gestalt is the Genius Loci."@en ;
    skos:note "John Keel's Strange Creatures from Time and Space is named for the Mothman case at an abandoned ammunition dump."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, p. 54; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnGeniusLociGestalt a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Genius loci as the gestalt of a site"@en, "Genius loci als Gestalt eines Ortes"@de ;
    skos:definition "The spirit of a place is the gestalt of its ecology (lie of the land, plant life, movements of animals, seasons, atmosphere, history, myth and story) together with the awareness of whoever enters it; any event there, battle, murder or rape, adds to its power, and some sites transmit an unwelcoming or even hostile atmosphere."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, p. 55; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnApproachingPlaceOfPower a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Approaching a place of power"@en, "Annäherung an einen Kraftort"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cast your senses widely; first keep quiet and listen; note the surroundings; go there at various times and spend a night if one can; approach it with respect as one would approach a powerful animal or person; mesh yourself into it rather than trying to control it."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, p. 55; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRaisingPowerAtSiteFailure a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Trying to raise power at a power site"@en, "Versuch, an einem Kraftort Macht zu erheben"@de ;
    skos:definition "Beginners sometimes err by attempting to raise power there; if it is truly a place of power, it cannot be controlled."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a condition of failure: of attempting to raise power at a place of power, a mistake of fledgling magicians, since a true place of power cannot be controlled. No deadline; the rate word is sometimes; the cause is put on the operator's approach."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, p. 55; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnEnteringAGate a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Entering a gate: perceiving the Old Ones as ever-present"@en, "Ein Tor betreten: die Alten als allgegenwärtig wahrnehmen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Finding and entering a gate as a state of consciousness rather than a ritual form, in which the Old Ones are perceived as ever-present, resembling the lore of Lovecraft's rustic folk."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, p. 55; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnHyperNostalgia a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Sustained directionless hyper-nostalgia"@en, "Anhaltende ziellose Hyper-Nostalgie"@de ;
    skos:definition "Through gateways such as Spare's pictures, angle webs, crystals, scrying mirrors, places or patterns, evoke Lovecraft's intense nostalgia for the long-vanished and maintain it over time without focusing it in any direction."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, Gateways, pp. 56-58; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnHyperNostalgiaYieldsRealmOfShadows a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Sustained hyper-nostalgia yields the shadow realm"@en, "Hyper-Nostalgie führt ins Schattenreich"@de ;
    skos:definition "Maintained without focus, hyper-nostalgia turns into a global feeling of moving among shadows, the Old Ones."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnHyperNostalgia ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRealmOfShadows ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, Gateways, pp. 56-58; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnRealmOfShadows a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Global sense of moving in a realm of shadows (the Old Ones)"@en, "Globales Gefühl, sich im Schattenreich zu bewegen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Turning from linear consciousness toward the tenuous, chaotic domain of the Great Old Ones."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Dark Zones, Gateways, pp. 56-58; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Appendices, Cthulhu Pathworking
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnCthulhuPathworkingYieldsTelepathicTransmission a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Cthulhu Pathworking yields telepathic sigil transmission"@en, "Cthulhu-Pfadarbeit sendet Sigillen telepathisch"@de ;
    skos:definition "The pathworking is said to create conditions for sigil enchantment and has been used to send telepathic messages over a wide area, Cthulhu as Lord of Dreams sending ripples when he stirs."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCthulhuPathworking ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTelepathicBroadcastOfSigil ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Cthulhu Pathworking, pp. 56-58; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnPreparingSigilForPathworking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Preparing a sigil before the pathworking"@en, "Ein Sigil vor der Pfadarbeit vorbereiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prepare a sigil of the desire to be sent before beginning the working."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Cthulhu Pathworking, pp. 56-58; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSigilBeforePathworkingPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Sigil prepared before the pathworking"@en, "Sigil vor der Pfadarbeit bereiten"@de ;
    skos:definition "A sigil should be prepared before beginning the pathworking."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnPreparingSigilForPathworking ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCthulhuPathworking ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Cthulhu Pathworking, pp. 56-58; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCthulhuPathworking a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Cthulhu Pathworking for sigil transmission"@en, "Cthulhu-Pfadarbeit zur Sigillensendung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Participants relax; a narrator guides: darkness and waves; standing on a beach under a sickly moon; walk into cold water up to the neck; will a change into a Deep One (or Shoggoth, left to each); hold breath, face fear of drowning, breathe the water; swim down past fish toward a glow; reach a cyclopean city with wrong, shifting angles and a heartbeat pulse; at the black monolith crowning Cthulhu's crypt, see the sigil glow, inhale, pause, exhale hurling it onto the monolith where it flashes and fades; a rumble and wave of energy carry you up to the surf in human form; return to the temple."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicPrcCExampleMythosDreamscapeSequenceRLyeh (Prime Chaos, 1999): the 1999 sequence is Hine's dreamscape for entering a personal dream-time; here a group pathworking descends to R'Lyeh in the shape of a Deep One and ends in hurling a prepared sigil against the monolith over Cthulhu's crypt, for enchantment."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Cthulhu Pathworking, pp. 58-60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .



#
# Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain
#

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTsathogguaNaming a pm:Naming ;
    rdfs:label "Tsathoggua"@en, "Tsathoggua"@de ;
    skos:definition "Protean toad-entity of Mythos fiction (Clark Ashton Smith, The Seven Geases), invoked by the author to swallow a sickness-spirit; Mythos entities linked by him to the limbic system or dragon brain."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondBToadSpiritNaming (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node names an unnamed larger toad-spirit by which Hine was possessed in a healing ceremony; here the entity is Tsathoggua of Clark Ashton Smith's fiction, and the possession partial."@en ;
    pm:nameForm "Tsathoggua" ;
    pm:nameRole "protean toad-entity; devourer of a lesser toad-spirit" ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnTranceInvestigationPsychicBody a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Trance-investigation of a psychic body"@en, "Trance-Untersuchung eines Psychokörpers"@de ;
    skos:definition "In trance, investigate the patient's psychic body to see the psychic root of an illness (here a bloated toad lodged in a friend's swollen neck glands)."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondBTranceDiagnosisToadPossessionHealing (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node tells the case in a few lines with an unnamed toad-spirit and a client; here the trance-investigation of a friend's psychic body opens a full account of preparation, invocation, swallowing and after-effects, naming Tsathoggua."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnSeeingPsychicRootOfIllness a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Seeing the psychic root of illness"@en, "Die psychische Wurzel einer Krankheit sehen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Perceiving an illness as a spirit-form (sickness-spirit toad)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnFamiliarSpiritBiggerToadReceiving a pm:Receiving ;
    rdfs:label "Familiar spirit: only a bigger toad will dislodge it"@en, "Familiargeist: nur eine größere Kröte hilft"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author was told by a familiar spirit that only an even bigger toad would dislodge the sickness-toad; he then used meditation and divination before deciding to invoke Tsathoggua."@en ;
    skos:note "Differs from pmp:ChaosMagicCondBTranceDiagnosisToadPossessionHealing (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node has his allies, asked in trance, say the throat spirit would heed only a larger toad; here a familiar spirit informs him that only a bigger toad would dislodge it, and he settles on Tsathoggua after meditation and divination."@en ;
    pm:receivedBy "Phil Hine" ;
    pm:claimedSpeaker "A familiar spirit, not named in the text" ;
    pm:receptionCircumstance "During protracted healing work for a friend with swollen neck glands, after investigating her psychic body in trance; Hine then used meditation and divination before deciding to invoke Tsathoggua." ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPreparationBeforeTsathogguaPrerequisite a pm:Presupposing ;
    rdfs:label "Due preparation before invoking Tsathoggua"@en, "Gebührende Vorbereitung vor der Tsathoggua-Anrufung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The author invoked Tsathoggua only after due preparation (sleep deprivation, fasting, dancing)."@en ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnPreparationSleepFastDance ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:ChaosMagicPsnTsathogguaHealingInvocation ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnTsathogguaInvocationYieldsHealingAct a pm:Yielding ;
    rdfs:label "Tsathoggua invocation yields healing act"@en, "Tsathoggua-Anrufung bewirkt Heilakt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Drawing on Tsathoggua's power was reasoned to enable commanding the sickness-toad; swallowing it broke the spell."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:ChaosMagicPsnTsathogguaHealingInvocation ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:ChaosMagicPsnHealingBySwallowingSicknessSpirit ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnPreparationSleepFastDance a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Preparation: sleep deprivation, fasting, drum dancing"@en, "Vorbereitung: Schlafentzug, Fasten, Trommeltanz"@de ;
    skos:definition "Prepare by fasting and going without sleep, together with energetic dancing to drumming the evening before."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 60; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmp:ChaosMagicPsnTsathogguaHealingInvocation a pm:Practicing ;
    rdfs:label "Tsathoggua healing invocation"@en, "Tsathoggua-Heilungsanrufung"@de ;
    skos:definition "Cast a circle with drum, rattle, bells and free chant; paint face white with ash and blood; bind the patient with meticulously knotted cords, whispering binding spells into the knots; visualise Tsathoggua squatting on its throne, oozing through tunnels, hopping among pillars; move about feeling one's outline as a huge toad, shifting balance and muttering ever more guttural identifications until partial possession; stop resisting; then mentally project the toad-tongue down the patient's throat to engulf and swallow the sickness-spirit; collapse and shed the toad-skin using a magical mask as focus; centre, release the patient, banish, continue with milder trancework."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine says the techniques of invocation drew on drama exercises (see pmc:ChaosMagicPsnInvocationFromDramaExercises)."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, pp. 60-61; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnPartialPossessionTsathogguaSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Signs heralding partial possession by Tsathoggua"@en, "Zeichen beginnender Teilbesessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Shifts of consciousness heralding partial possession: copious salivation, tongue seeming to swell, legs refusing to hold him upright, inability to oppose thumbs, black-and-white haze over vision, moments submerged in toad-ness with nausea, agony and exultation; on ceasing to resist, a bifurcated awareness observing and directing the body."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of partial possession by Tsathoggua, heralded by copious salivation, a swelling tongue, legs that would not bear him, thumbs that would not oppose, a black-and-white haze, and then a split awareness observing the body. Judged by the operator alone; no deadline; the healing is said to have broken the spell, and the friend's recovery is not reported."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnHealingBySwallowingSicknessSpirit a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Healing by commanding and swallowing a sickness-spirit"@en, "Heilung durch Verschlucken eines Krankheitsgeistes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Taking on Tsathoggua's aspect and power to command a sickness-spirit to leave, or to swallow it; the swallowing is said to have broken the spell."@en ;
    skos:note "The text says the spell was broken; it does not report the friend's physical recovery."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnIngestingSicknessSpiritsCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "After-effects of ingesting sickness-spirits"@en, "Nachwirkungen des Verschluckens von Krankheitsgeistern"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the working the author slept about ten hours and woke with severe stomach cramps and vomiting; nausea lasted about three days; such after-effects of ingesting sickness-spirits are, in his experience, not unusual."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnTsathogguaHealingInvocation ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, p. 61; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnMythosEntitiesLimbicBrain a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Mythos entities and the limbic \"dragon brain\"; rare direct use"@en, "Mythos-Wesen und das limbische \"Drachenhirn\""@de ;
    skos:definition "The working is said to further confirm a link between Mythos entities and the limbic system, the dragon brain; frogs and toads are sources of wisdom in many myths; the author has rarely used a Mythos entity for a direct result like healing."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:firstPersonReport ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, Comments, p. 62; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAnimalPossessionIndicatorsSign a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Indicators of animal-spirit possession: vision and face change"@en, "Anzeichen von Tiergeist-Besessenheit"@de ;
    skos:definition "Correspondence with shamanic workers suggests that animal-spirit possession is commonly marked by changes in visual perception and in the face."@en ;
    skos:note "Counted as a sign of success: of animal-spirit possession, whose common positive indicators are said to be changes in visual perception and in physiognomy. Reported from unnamed correspondents in shamanic work; the change of face is visible to others, the change of vision only to the possessed."@en, "Differs from pmc:ChaosMagicCondCFacialChangeSuccess (Condensed Chaos, 1995): the 1995 node makes marked facial change a positive indicator of invocation generally; here changes of vision and of physiognomy are indicators of animal-spirit possession, reported from correspondents in shamanic work."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, Comments, p. 62; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnInvocationFromDramaExercises a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Invocatory technique taken from drama exercises (Johnstone, Impro)"@en, "Invokationstechnik aus Theaterübungen (Johnstone, Impro)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Hine says the techniques of invocation in the Tsathoggua working drew on drama exercises, some of them found in Keith Johnstone's Impro, and that the name Tsathoggua itself helps when shifting from plain speech into toad-like sounds."@en ;
    skos:note "Keith Johnstone's Impro is named as the source of some of the exercises; Hine does not describe them."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, Comments, p. 62; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .

pmc:ChaosMagicPsnAbyssGazesBackCautela a pm:Cautioning ;
    rdfs:label "The abyss gazes back (Nietzsche)"@en, "Der Abgrund blickt zurück (Nietzsche)"@de ;
    skos:definition "Closing epigraph: whoever gazes into the abyss is gazed into by it in return."@en ;
    skos:note "Hine ascribes this to a work he names and does not cite by edition: Friedrich Nietzsche, quoted as the closing epigraph without naming the work. The node holds what The Pseudonomicon reports; the named work is reachable in its own right and is not read here, so the node is entered as Hine's ascription and not as a mediated reading of that work."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:ChaosMagicPsnCourtingMadness ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:thirdPartyAscription ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:ChaosMagic ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:ThePseudonomicon ;
    dcterms:source "Hine, The Pseudonomicon (Tempe, AZ: New Falcon, 2004, third revised edition, third printing 2007; first edition Chaos International, 1994), Appendices, Old Toad Under A Mountain, Comments, p. 62; read in a full-text layer of the third printing" .
